Meaningful Remarks by Samdech Techo Hun Sen on the Occasion of the 41st Anniversary of the January 7 Victory Day (9-Page Speech)
(Phnom Penh): On the occasion of the solemn celebration of the 41st anniversary of the January 7 Victory Day, the people gathered at the Koh Pich Center this morning, January 7, 2020, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, President of the Cambodian People's Party Delivered the most meaningful speech.
The following is a complete nine-page speech by Samdech Techo Hun Sen in front of tens of thousands of people:
This morning, more than 30,000 people gathered at Koh Pich to celebrate the 41st anniversary of Resurrection Day
(Phnom Penh): January 7, 2020 is the 41st anniversary of the January 7 Victory Day, the day that Cambodians across the country were freed from the dead of the Democratic Kampuchea regime led by the genocidal group Pol Pot Khieu Samphan Eang. Sary and Nuon Chea that cruel.
This morning, more than 30,000 Cambodians will gather at the Koh Pich Center to celebrate their Victory Day. Samdech Techo Hun Sen, President of the Cambodian People's Party, and Samdech Ponhea Chakrei Heng Samrin, Honorary President of the Cambodian People's Party, will preside over the celebration of this great day for the nation and the people of Cambodia.
January 7, 1979 was the day that Khmer fighters under the leadership of the Cambodian People's Party, including Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Samdech Ponhea Chakrei Heng Samrin, Samdech Thamma Pothisal Chea Sim, and many other senior CPP figures, overthrew him. Successfully overthrew the Khmer Rouge regime, the deadly Democratic Kampuchea regime.
Cambodians across the country consider January 7, 1979, the day of the historic victory of the Cambodian people, the reopening of a new era: independence, freedom, democracy and social progress. It was not until January 7, 1979 that the Cambodian people were revived and a step forward for the Cambodian nation to evolve from a hellish state to a prosperous and prosperous country. Currently.
In the 41 years since the fall of the Khmer Rouge regime under the leadership of the Cambodian People's Party, Samdech Techo Hun Sen as the head of countless national achievements has occurred. The nation of Cambodia has transformed from a ruined country, transformed from a pile of ashes into a land of prosperity, vibrancy and beauty, and is moving forward vigorously towards a glorious future.
During these 41 years, Cambodia has achieved many great historical achievements, such as:
First, prevent the return of the genocidal regime and regain the rights, freedoms, honor, dignity and democracy that were completely lost in the genocidal regime.
Due to the win-win policy, there is a great historical value that the government has designated "December 29, 1998 as the day to end the civil war in Cambodia through win-win politics" and built a win-win monument to commemorate and Preserve symbols and artifacts that show the history of the national liberation struggle and the achievements of building and defending the motherland as a memory and a lesson for all generations.
Cambodia is considered one of the fastest growing economies in the world and one of the largest economies in Asia, maintaining an average growth rate of more than 7% over the last two decades, reflected by the rapid growth in the manufacturing industry. Agricultural construction, tourism services, real estate, transportation and transportation.
The volume of investment in both the public and private sectors has increased significantly. Roads, bridges, electricity, water, irrigation, hospitals, schools have been restored and rebuilt all over the country. Factories, farms, business centers, high-rise buildings are being built more and more, employing millions of people everywhere.
(Phnom Penh): On January 7, 2020, the 41st National Victory Day on January 7, the whole nation and the people of Cambodia will celebrate Victory Day throughout the country and there will be tens of thousands of people celebrating at the Koh Pich Center to commemorate From the revival of the Cambodian nation and the survival of the Cambodian people.
