1: Vietnam
Currently, China and the United States are stuck in a trade war and there is no certainty to end it. Indeed, the clash between the two world economic powers could be the cause of the global economic crisis. Vietnam is not worried. Vietnam, a manufacturing hub on China's southern border, will certainly benefit.
Vietnam's strategic location next to China and the presence of several seaports make it easy to relocate existing supply chains here as well. Meanwhile, with a population of nearly 100 million, Vietnam's fast-growing middle class economy is growing as fast as China's 30 years ago.
The Philippines is more similar to Vietnam than any other country on our list. Both countries are large, high-growth markets with a population of about 90 million and a growing middle class. Each country has been experiencing economic growth of more than 5% per year for some time.
But unlike Vietnam, the Philippine peso is one of the most active currencies in Asia. One-year term deposits generally offer higher interest rates of 6% per annum. Therefore, banking in the Philippines is an increasingly popular option for investors looking to profit from their free money.
Priests send condolences to the families of the victims and the families of the victims of the Kep building collapse
According to the condolence message, the Board of Directors expressed its deep regret and sadness at the recent incident of the collapse of a seven-storey building in Kep province on January 3, 2020, which caused the deaths and injuries of workers. Some injuries. The Board of Directors considers this to be the most unfortunate and tragic tragedy that we all do not want to see, do not want to hear and do not want to meet at all.
The condolence message reads: "The Board of Priests would like to extend our deepest condolences and deepest condolences to the victims and the most emotional and sympathetic with the group. The families of the bodies and the families of all the victims. "Let the souls of the victims who lost their lives go to the grave in peace, do not mourn."
"I wish all the injured victims who are recovering well to be healthy, strong and recover from their injuries." The Board of Directors emphasizes further.
In that, the Board of Priests wholeheartedly commended Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, for sacrificing his physical, mental, material and intellectual strength without fear of fatigue and indefatigability in rescuing the victims of this incident. And mourn with the people everywhere and at all times.
The Board of Directors wishes Samdech Techo Hun Sen and Samdech Kittipritthbandit Bun Rany Hun Sen, as well as their sons, daughters and grandchildren good health, long life, wisdom and success in the cause of serving the motherland and Buddhism.
