(Kampong Chhnang): Hand-woven bamboo is a legacy of the ancestors that farmers living in Pat Lang village, Kraing Leav commune, Rolea Bier district continue to maintain and protect and to this day to improve their daily lives after the snooze business. Of farming.
This is because they know about the trees that grow in the fields and at the mouth of streams, lakes, etc. Currently, they are worried because the place where he has to go to pluck the weeds to weave the weeds has been cleared, and in the future, the weeds will not be able to weave the weeds anymore.
A woman who lives in Pat Lang village, Krang Leav commune, said that she has been weaving and selling bean curd for almost 20 years to improve her livelihood and earn money for her children to study. On average, he weaves 4 to 6 stitches a day, or more if he works hard. More than 100 families in Pat Lang village, Kraing Leav commune, have been engaged in the weaving business, which they have been doing since ancient times.
The farmers who weave the bongyi confirmed that the business of weaving bongki for sale does not cost capital, only the cost of riding a bullock cart, with one riel for each person being paid 3,000 riel. In addition to pruning trees, saplings are also used to make thnong bongki.
Mr. Khuong Sreng brought Red Cross donations to 175 families affected by house fire in Sangkat Toul Sangke I
(Phnom Penh): If people have any urgent problems, please go to the nearest CPP office, party officials are waiting to be dealt with immediately. But if you do not dare to enter the CPP office, please go to the commune or district hall, there will be officials waiting to solve it for you.
Phnom Penh Governor Khuong Sreng said this on the evening of January 6, 2020, while distributing gifts to 175 families affected by fire in Bak Touk village, Sangkat Tuol Sangke 1, Khan Russey Keo, Phnom Penh.
Mr. Khuong Sreng continued that Samdech Kittipritthbandit Bun Rany Hun Sen, President of the Cambodian Red Cross, after receiving the above bad news, immediately introduced to the Phnom Penh Red Cross Branch to bring gifts to distribute to the people immediately. Is distributed before sunset on January 6.
He added that upon receiving this instruction, the Phnom Penh Red Cross Branch immediately visited and gave gifts to all 175 families. In addition, Mr. Khuong Sreng also asked the district authorities to continue to take care of these victims, if they have any problems must come down to help him immediately.
On that occasion, Mr. Khuong Sreng, Chairman of the Branch Committee of the Cambodian Red Cross in Phnom Penh, brought a message from Samdech Kittipritthbandit Bun Rany Hun Sen to all the people who suffered from fire.
After that, the President of Phnom Penh Branch expressed his deepest sympathy and condolences to the 175 families whose houses were destroyed by the fire and their property was destroyed. However, we were able to save our lives. To continue living and working hard as well as working to have the resources to continue living a better life.
The President of the Phnom Penh Red Cross Branch also instructed the local authorities to facilitate all relevant documents such as residence book, ID card, marriage certificate or other documents so that all 175 families have the opportunity to find a job as well as set aside for other uses. More.
In addition, the President called on people to be very careful and check the flammable appliances and turn off the flames to prevent flames, it can destroy personal property and waste time, as the saying goes. "Submerge in the middle of the river to prevent house fires."
At the same time, the Governor also asked the Water Supply Authority to facilitate the water supply to the people who are short of water now.
