(Phnom Penh): The First Vice President of the National Assembly of Cambodia has expressed his desire to see a settlement between Bangladesh and Myanmar regarding the peaceful influx of Rofingia migrants into Bangladesh.
The inauguration of the First Vice President of the National Assembly was held as he allowed a delegation from the Standing Committee on Foreign Affairs of the Parliament of Bangladesh to participate in the morning of January 6, 2020 at the National Assembly.
The Bangladeshi delegation asked Cambodia to help resolve and put pressure on Myanmar to allow Rofia migrants to live in Bangladesh.
He said more than 1.1 million Rohingya were currently living in Bangladesh. Bangladesh has also held talks with neighboring countries, asking them to help refugees return to Myanmar effectively and peacefully.
He added that Bangladesh would be happy if any talks were held soon to allow the Rohingya to return home soon. Cambodia and Bangladesh are also friends with Myanmar, so Bangladesh hopes that Cambodia will put pressure on Myanmar to ensure that the Rohingya return to the country to live in peace and with a dignified economy.
Bangladesh and Cambodia have had a long relationship. In the past, there have also been visits between senior officials as well as the Prime Minister. He said that the economic sectors of Cambodia and Bangladesh have economic growth of more than 7%, which will cause the two countries to grow faster in the future.
During the meeting, Dr. Nguon Nhel said that Cambodia hopes that Bangladesh and Myanmar will resolve the issue of migrants peacefully.
"As for Cambodia's position on the issue of Rofia immigration to Bangladesh, we hope that both sides will hold a bilateral peace talks and achieve their respective requests," he said.
He said the Cambodian parliament fully supports the initiation of peaceful bilateral talks. At the same time, Dr. also asked Bangladesh to attract more tourists to visit Cambodia because Cambodia has full peace and great potential in tourism. In terms of economy and trade, the Cambodian parliament also hopes that the Bangladeshi parliament will encourage more investors to come to Cambodia.
