(Phnom Penh): To ensure that school food is free of chemicals and hygienic and to ensure that students are not adversely affected by school food, especially schools in the provinces and remote areas of the Ministry of Education and Youth And Sports have released a press release on a school food safety campaign.
According to a press release obtained by Fresh News on January 4, 2019, the Ministry has issued Instruction No. 18 IKSON dated May 2, 2019 on strengthening enforcement measures. Promoting food safety and well-being in public and private schools, where food is banned in educational institutions, such as:
1: All kinds of expired foods 2: Alcohol and all kinds of tobacco products 3: Foods of unknown origin 4: Energy drinks, very sweet drinks, coffee, ice cream, syrup, all 5: Chocolate, candy And all kinds of gum 6: all kinds of jelly, donuts and very sweet cakes.
At the same time, the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports, in collaboration with MSA and KBSK, will conduct a campaign on food safety in schools from January 1 to 31, 2020.
The main purpose of this campaign is to widely disseminate to key stakeholders such as the school management committee or school principals and food vendors in all schools so that they have high attention and control. Thoroughly inspect all school meals.
The campaign will educate vendors, who are the main targets, to be aware of the rules of hygiene in the sale and ban on the sale of food that can cause health problems for students, especially young children.
The first phase of the campaign will start at Preah Norodom Primary School in Daun Penh, Phnom Penh, with the participation of Dr. Hang Chuon Naron, Minister of Education, on January 6, 2020 at 8 am and in Angkor Primary School is located in Kang Meas District, Kampong Cham Province on January 9, 2020 at 1:30 pm.
