Ms. Men Neary Sopheak pointed out that the Cambodian Red Cross has been implementing the Road Safety and Disability Project since 2005 and has received financial support from many donors, including the private sector. This project is part of the orientation of youth and people in terms of contributing to reduce the number of deaths, injuries and disabilities due to traffic accidents.
Svay Rieng Provincial Governor Presents Tablets to Communes in Provinces for Implementing Cash Support Program for Pregnant Women and Children Under 2
(Svay Rieng): Mr. Men Vibol, Governor of Svay Rieng Province, on December 30, 2019, presided over the handing over ceremony of tablets to communes in Svay Rieng province for the implementation of cash support program for pregnant women and children under 2 years old. Poor identification on request.
In Cambodia, forests have been severely deforested in recent years. Some of the companies that received economic land concessions from the Royal Government were only logging and did not develop or cultivate anything, which makes them seem less confident when it comes to agricultural development. Wood of Sing Biotech.
Mr. Hulpo said that the timber at the developer site is degraded and cannot be used for economic purposes. So clearing and cultivating wood for processing is a good thing.
Mr. Hulpo also confirmed the company's tree planting activities. Hulpo said he had never been to a developer site, but that he had seen trees, such as teak and oil palm trees planted on the company's site. .
In addition to confirming the company's tree planting activities, the people in the area also confirmed the state of the forest on the land that the company is developing. Both Mr. Yeung Sokhy and Mr. Hul Po confirmed that the forest on the development company land was degraded, with no large trees. Previously, there were large trees on the land, but they were cut down by the people before the company came to develop.
Many countries in the world, including neighboring Cambodia, have developed a wood-planting industry to make plywood or furniture such as tables, cabinets, chairs, beds, etc. for the office and home. For Cambodia, no investment company has invested in planting trees for such processing, only natural loggers to sell abroad.
Sing Bay Otec is the first company in Cambodia to invest in plantation wood to be processed into plywood or furniture. Lu Chu Chhang, Director of Sing Bay Otec (Cambodia), said that Cambodia has high potential and a large area of land, which is highly favorable for the cultivation of wood for processing into furniture.
Lu Zhouchang claims that the market for this furniture is very large today, such as in Taiwan, South Korea and some other countries. Mr. Lu Chuchang said that for Cambodia, it is a good opportunity to think about the wood industry and process the furniture for domestic use and export.
The director of Sing Bay Otec encourages the development of the plantation industry and he is happy to share his experience with those who want to invest in the industry.
Mr. Veng Sakhon, Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, expressed his strong support for Singbiotec's plantation. According to the Minister, Cambodia needs to import not less than 300,000 cubic meters of plywood per year to be used in the construction sector. National economy.
Regarding the investment in plantations in Cambodia, Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State and spokesman for the Ministry of Environment also expressed support and said that investment in plantations is very important because it will reduce the pressure on the use of wood. Naturally, the establishment of this wood processing industry will also help create jobs and increase the income of the people in the area to participate in the national economic growth.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra stated that the establishment of plantations is an effective strategy to prevent natural resource crime and contribute to strengthening the conservation of natural resources that Cambodia has today.
Sing Bay Otec not only cleared the land and planted seedlings to support tree planting projects to support the wood processing industry, the company also established a large scientific research and breeding institute in Kampong Thom province on an area of approximately 37 hectares. The institute plans to produce about 10 million seedlings a year.
In addition to the current tree planting efforts, the company also has two wood processing plants for wood chips. One location on National Road 6 in Muk Kampoul district, Kandal province is a finished product factory and the other in Kampong Cham province is a semi-finished product factory.
Sing Bay Otec's afforestation investment has not only contributed to the national economy through the payment of taxes, but also helped the people in both the afforestation investment area in Kratie province and the people at the processing plant site in Kampong Cham and Kandal provinces get jobs and earn money to support their livelihoods as well.
Residents in Kratie province's Sambo district, near a logging company in a remote area, depend on logging, fishing and farming to support their livelihoods. These jobs do not allow them to earn a regular income. When Syng Biotech went into plantation, it provided many jobs for the people and also improved their lives.
Mr. Soeung Yun is a resident of Tonle Bit Village, Tonle Bit Commune, Tbong Khmum District, Tbong Khmum Province. Previously, Soeung Yun and his wife migrated to work in Thailand. Currently, Mr. Soeung Yun and his wife have moved to work in the wood processing factory of Sing Biotech in Kampong Cham province.
Mr. Soeung Yun is satisfied with working in a factory, earning a salary of 2 million riel per month, close to home, close to his children, and he also spends time raising animals. Soeung Yun claims that working in the country and close to home saves him more money than going to work in Thailand.
