BEIJING (Reuters) - China's customs authority on Monday said it had decided to ban the import of pigs, wild boar and pork-related products from Indonesia due to African swine fever. This is according to the release by CNA on Monday evening, December 30, 2019.
On its website, the Chinese Customs Authority also said that 392 cases of swine fever were reported in Indonesia on December 17. However, China has not specified how long the ban will last, as the disease has been spreading in China, forcing Chinese authorities to kill millions of pigs since the plague. It first erupted across the country in August 2018.
