(Phnom Penh): All countries, as well as Cambodia today, are actively paying attention to the protection of the rights and safety of minors. Because some potentially dangerous situations for minors are the result of the misconduct of individuals through various forms in order to exploit and endanger those children.
Minors are those who are underage or under the age of 18 and are vulnerable to social abuse due to limited cognitive ability and physical strength. To show what kind of activities can be manifested by the above wickedness? And to what extent should the perpetrators be punished according to the law?
Fresh News Legal Awareness Program in collaboration with the Cambodian Lawyers for International Cooperation would like to raise the topic of "Putting Minors Under Danger (Health and Psychology)" to share and share with students. All students are as follows:
In order to prevent and solve the problem of danger to children, the Criminal Code of the Kingdom of Cambodia clearly stipulates the activities that are considered to put the juvenile in a dangerous situation both physically and mentally and has set the following aggravating circumstances: :
The main activity that can endanger the life and development of minors is fasting or lack of care for minors under 15 years of age.
At the same time, in the event that the action under Article 337 may cause the victim to die, the aggravating circumstance will increase to imprisonment from 7 (seven) years to 15 (fifteen) years, based on Article 338. Of the same law.
Another activity that can lead to crime is putting minors in working conditions that cause health damage. Examples of such tasks include lifting heavy objects, performing work without proper rest, and working in areas where there is smoke or toxic odors.
1. Ancestors 2. A person who has power over a minor 3. Many individuals who act as perpetrators, co-conspirators, initiators or accomplices. Payment of dividends.
In summary, any person who commits any act that could put a minor under a health and psychological hazard shall be held accountable before the law. At the same time, parents or guardians, as well as society as a whole, should increase the attention paid to minors through education and guidance on self-protection.
