1. How does a solar eclipse occur?
An eclipse can only occur during the Earth's orbit and the Moon around the Sun. The three objects reach a point where they are aligned in a straight line with the Sun and the Earth in place. One side of the moon. Generally, there are between 2 and 5 solar eclipses a year.
* Full solar eclipse: It occurs when we look at the earth, the moon obscures the sun almost immediately. This type of solar eclipse can only be seen in a small area on Earth.
Incomplete solar eclipse: It occurs when we look from the earth, the sun is not obscured by the moon, it can occur below 100%, the sun is still just softer. A bit like the case that just happened in Cambodia today.
