The celebration of the winning school, which was held at the Diakonia Center on December 23, was attended by officials from the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports, principals and teachers, as well as company representatives. Prudential Cambodia and the Prudential Foundation and representatives of the Children's Rights Foundation.
Three schools have been selected and recognized by Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports officials based on the benefits and support their students receive through the implementation of the Cha-Ching program to help develop their financial literacy skills. More.
The Cha-Ching program provides learning and practice materials for teachers and parents to help teach their children how to spend money. The program is also introduced to many other schools through educational programs with volunteers from Prudential Cambodia participating directly with students, who often dress up as Cha-Ching cartoon characters. .
"We are extremely proud of the Cha-Ching program and the positive response and enthusiasm from students, parents and teachers," said Seang Leakena, Senior Director of Community Development and Support at Prudential Cambodia. We would also like to thank the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports for its support in launching this program in public schools in Cambodia.
They shared their views with us that literacy, finance and budget management skills are very important for primary school children and can give them a bright future as they earn money, save money and save money. Donate and these skills contribute to improving the well-being of their lives and also promote the development of Cambodia.
Soeng Chhun, Deputy Director of the Chbar Ampov District Education Office and a judge of the Cha-Ching Program, said: "Due to the change in attitude (in money management), I would like to see this program expanded throughout the country. We request the Ministry of Education and partners to continue to support this program.
