Senior official of the Ministry of Environment leads a consultative meeting in Prey Veng province to include Boeung Sneh wetland as a protected area
(Prey Veng): Mr. Srun Darith, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Environment, this morning, December 26, 2019, presided over the opening ceremony of the Provincial Consultation on "Incorporation of Boeung Sneh Wetland as a Protected Area" in the province. Prey Veng.
The meeting was also attended by Ms. Leang Srey Mol, Deputy Governor of Prey Veng Province, leaders, professional officers of the Ministry of Environment, relevant ministries, institutions, provincial administrations, district administrations, local authorities and community representatives.
Speaking on the occasion, Ms. Leang Srey Mol said that the ecosystem of Boeung Sneh area has played an important role and is a source of water for the people living in more than 14 communes who use the lake to irrigate more than tens of thousands of hectares of dry rice. Can grow 2 to 3 times a year.
She said that in the past, there has been an increase in the population living around Boeung Sneh, an increase in demand for firewood and encroachment on flooded forest land for agriculture.
At the same time, Mr. Srun Darith said that some species of birds are endangered and endangered in the world, such as the gray squirrel, the squirrel, the small squirrel, and especially the species that are reproducing snails. Thousands of eggs hatch at the Tali Pantali point of the Boeung Sneh wetland. The Ministry of Environment aims to conserve the biodiversity of these birds for the benefit of an important eco-tourism destination for the community.
According to the Secretary of State, based on economic value and biodiversity value, the provincial administration has requested the cooperation of the Ministry of Environment to include the Boeung Sneh wetland as a nature reserve to improve the livelihood of local communities as well as use by The wisdom and sustainability of this wetland resource. Through this work, the potential of Boeung Sneh area will be involved in protecting natural resources and developing local communities.
The Ministry of Environment has unanimously supported the inclusion of the Boeung Sneh wetland as a nature reserve to ensure the protection and conservation of the natural resources of the Boeung Sneh wetland for the benefit and development of local communities. As well as for mankind, both now and in the future.
It should be noted that today's discussion was organized for the following purposes: 1: Share information about the potential of Boeung Sneh wetland located in Prey Veng province and 2: Discuss and receive additional recommendations from stakeholders on the wetland inclusion process. This lake of love has become a nature reserve.
Afterwards, participants will be able to see first-hand the potential of wetlands and the habitat of birds in the field. More than 20,000 patients will receive TB treatment services by the end of 2019.
(Phnom Penh): The President of the National Center for Tuberculosis and Hepatitis Control, Mr. Mao Tan Eang, stated that by the end of 2019, 22,000 patients have received TB treatment services at health centers and referral hospitals throughout. Country.
"Deaths have dropped dramatically in recent years, with between 500,000 and 100,000 people successfully receiving treatment and tens of thousands more dying," he said. "The death rate from tuberculosis has dropped by 57%, while the new disease rate, called encephalitis, has dropped by 48%, which is more than any other country."
His remarks were made at the opening of a consultative workshop on the National Strategic Plan to End TB 2021-2030 recently, chaired by Professor Eng Huot, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Health.
Mr. Mao Tan Eang stated that this meeting was to discuss the new plan document, the Strategic Plan to End TB in Cambodia 2021-2030, to find out the key activities that are priorities and comprehensive measures to be taken. Reduce morbidity and mortality in line with the Sustainable Development Goals of the Royal Government of Cambodia as well as the international community to reduce TB mortality by up to 90% and Illness by 80% by the end of 2030.
Despite the decline in TB deaths and illnesses, the number of TB cases in Cambodia is still high, so call on all stakeholders, including our people. Get rid of TB. In particular, patients need to receive regular treatment according to the doctor's schedule, because this disease requires a little longer to heal.
In addition, people who have symptoms of cough, fever, sweating, weight loss for more than 2 weeks and lymph nodes, please hurry to the health center or referral hospital near you for free treatment. Un Chanda encourages Cambodians with disabilities to work hard to earn a living, please do not give up on mental health
