In addition, the technological and military development activities of some major countries are also very interesting during 2019. The following are the international political and security events that took place from the beginning of January 2019 until the end of December 2019, which are as follows:
February 1 - US President Donald Trump acknowledges that Washington will withdraw from the 1987 INF nuclear deal with Russia, and days after Moscow suspends its obligations under the treaty.
February 12 - The Republic of Macedonia was renamed the Northern Republic of Macedonia.
February 14 - A suicide bomb attack on an Indian military convoy in Kashmir kills 40 Indian soldiers, the deadliest attack on Indian forces in Kashmir in 30 years. This.
February 26 and 27: The Indian Air Force launches air strikes on militant bases it accuses of being behind the February 14 bombings in Balakot, Pakistan. . The attack escalated military tensions between the two countries, leading to claims of shooting down their respective fighter jets.
February 27 and 28: The United States and North Korea hold the second summit between Donald Trump and Kim Jong Un in Hanoi, Vietnam. But the two leaders ended the meeting without issuing a joint statement or with any major results as tensions erupted again to this day.
* March 24 - General elections in Thailand result in the victory of Prayuth Chan-o-cha's party, and Prayuth is elected by the National Assembly to become Prime Minister.
May 12 - A cargo ship capsizes in the Gulf of Oman, hitting two Saudi oil tankers, while Washington and Riyadh accuse Iran of being behind the attack, but Iran denies it. .
May 17 - Taiwan becomes the first region in Asia to legalize same-sex marriage.
May 23 - Narendra Modi re-elected Prime Minister of India
May 27 - US President Donald Trump pays a state visit to Japan and meets with the new emperor.
June 9 - More than a million Hong Kong protesters begin a protest against the extradition of suspects to mainland China.
June 18 - The United States sends 1,000 more troops to the Middle East, defending its allies from the Iranian threat.
20th to 21st: Chinese President Xi Jinping visits North Korea.
June 30 - Donald Trump becomes the first US president to cross the border from South Korea into North Korea to meet with Kim Jong Un.
July 1 - Japan announces restrictions on exports of key technology equipment for the production of mobile phone screens, smartphones and chips to South Korea. Demonstrators protest against the Hong Kong government on the occasion of the anniversary of the return of Hong Kong from Britain to China (July 1, 1997).
September 4 - The Hong Kong government announces the repeal of the extradition law to mainland China.
October 1 - China celebrates the 70th anniversary of the founding of the People's Republic of China. Hong Kong police use live ammunition to shoot a protester for the first time.
October 4 - The Hong Kong government bans the wearing of masks or masks during protests, sparking outrage.
October 5 - More than 90 protesters are killed in violent protests in Iraq against the economic downturn.
November 27 - The Donald Trump administration passes the Hong Kong Law on Human Rights and Democracy, but China accuses it of "serious interference in China's internal affairs."
December 5 - The House Justice Committee files two articles to prosecute Donald Trump.
December 18 - US Congress approves two articles accusing Donald Trump of abuse of power and obstructing congressional investigations, making him the third president. In the history of the United States, the process of removal was carried out.
