(VOVworld) - The Cambodian Red Cross branch in Ratanakkiri province and the Yalai Red Cross Association of the Socialist Republic of Vietnam signed a cooperation agreement on humanitarian affairs this morning.
The signing ceremony of the agreement on humanitarian operations took place at Ratanakkiri Provincial Hall between Mr. Thong Savun, Chairman of the Ratanakkiri Provincial Committee of the Cambodian Red Cross and Mr. Yoeung Dinh Yean, President of the Yalai Provincial Red Cross Association.
Mr. Thong Savun stated that according to the content of the international cooperation agreement between the Socialist Republic of Vietnam and the National Red Cross Society and the functions, duties and content of the cooperation between the central level of the Cambodian Red Cross and the Central Committee of the Vietnamese Red Cross. To the signing ceremony between the Cambodian Red Cross branch in Ratanakkiri province and the Yalai Red Cross Association.
This agreement is made in accordance with the basic principles and statutes of the International Movement of the Red Cross and Red Crescent, the 1997 Seville Agreement, the strategy of the three national associations, Cambodia, Laos, Vietnam, as well as national and international laws and regulations currently in force. The spirit of equality, respect for the system, with the vision, regulations and traditions of the host country.
The main contents of the agreement are: cooperation on humanitarian affairs, the organization of meetings once a year by taking turns to host, all programs need to pay attention to the humanitarian cooperation of the two provinces: Emergency work, health care, promotion of humanitarian values, blood donation, disaster prevention.
When a disaster occurs, affecting the lives, health and property of the people, if there is a request from one party, the other party must assign an emergency rescue team. In addition, the two sides increase communication, exchange of all cultural, artistic and sports activities, as well as participation in the development experience of the Red Cross Association of the two provinces.
