The negative effects of the protests were not only on the socio-economic level, but also on the political level, which has exacerbated hostilities between Beijing and Hong Kong, as Beijing has warned against playing with fire. Turning to the economy of tourism, just the protest to close the airport on August 12 and 13, 2019, lost about 76 million US dollars.
The Hong Kong municipal government, on the other hand, only paid for hiring police officers to work overtime during the protests, which reached HK $ 950 million.
Protests by youth groups in Hong Kong, China, have been heading for six months, with protesters clashing frequently with municipal police, injuring both protesters and Hong Kong police officers. What they want to know is how this half-year-old story can be solved? Who is behind this sequel?
Last August, protesters, mostly young and bloodthirsty, took to the streets like a sea of people, until organizers said the number could rise to the millions. Authorities say the figure does not appear to be in the millions.
During this transition, Hong Kong was completely liberated under British colonial rule, and the city was ceded to China in 1997, and since then the promise of the Beijing administration has remained. On more paper.
Almost every day of protests by young people to students at the University of Technology in Hong Kong, not only the roads were temporarily closed until the air travel was suspended. The loss was almost non-existent in the past few weeks.
Since the June protests, about 6,100 people have been arrested in connection with various crimes, including attending unauthorized gatherings.
The number of arrests does not end there, which is expected to increase as Hong Kong police continue to search for evidence to charge the participants.
What is most interesting is the proposed law on the extradition of Hong Kong convicts to China, which has attracted the attention of people from almost all walks of life, including lawyers and doctors. As a sign, the people of this city seem to want more autonomy than sending fate to Beijing alone.
Behind the scenes, protesters have called for a clear investigation into allegations of police brutality, with protesters calling for more democracy. At this point, the heat seems to be back again, causing an increase in protesters who have almost declared a state of emergency in Hong Kong.
How has the six-month protest changed Hong Kong?
While Hong Kong was part of the People's Republic of China, the demand for autonomy also increased. As a former British colony, it enjoyed its own political and legal system, protecting the freedom of association. Expressing freedom of assembly and so on.
When Typhoon Hayatra erupted in June, demanding the removal of all extradition laws, it was threatened under the guise of maintaining social order, while Hong Kong, led by CEO Carrie Lam. Introduced a draft law that could allow the extradition of perpetrators to mainland China.
At the root of the problem are young people, including students, who have been pushing for protests in Hong Kong for years, since Hurricane Hayatra in 2012, which helped thwart plans to introduce love education. Chinese-style nationalism until the pro-democracy umbrella movement in 2014, compared to then and now, differs in the city of about 7 million people.
