Trade tensions between the two sides erupted after the European Commission concluded in March that palm oil was causing excessive deforestation and should not be considered a sustainable product. It will not be counted as a renewable resource and will stop refining it into biofuels for the transport sector between 2023 and 2030.
In addition, since August 2016, Indonesia has taken steps to restrict EU dairy products by blocking the passage of new imports and threatening to raise tariffs. Ms Malmstrom said taking the case to the World Trade Organization (WTO) was the only way to resolve the dispute between the two sides.
According to the European Commission, the EU biofuel market is worth about 9 billion euros ($ 13.5 billion) a year, with imports from Indonesia worth about 400 million euros. Last year, the EU consumed more than 7 million tonnes of palm oil, of which 65% was used for energy.
An official with the Indonesian Ministry of Commerce in April 2019 acknowledged that there had been a delay in licensing the import of alcohol from Europe, but insisted it was not in retaliation for EU oil plans. No, because it comes from marketing issues.
Vincent Piket, EU ambassador to Indonesia and Brunei, said free trade talks and complaints to the WTO were separate issues and urged both sides to work together. Second, adhere to WTO rules on trade.
According to Indonesian food industry officials, Europe supplies about 30 percent of Indonesia's dairy market, adding that importers can find new suppliers, although product specifications and contracts can be complicated.
In addition, Joko Supriyono, President of the Indonesian Palm Oil Association, recently told reporters that the European threat is very serious for the Indonesian palm oil industry, so Indonesia must be brave. In the use of trade instruments to retaliate against European agricultural products.
Conservatives of Khmer literature continue to ask Cambodians to use and preserve the national literature well
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Khemarak Bunna, the curator of the Khmer script, is still working hard to urge the Cambodian people to use the national literature as well as to preserve the cultural heritage of the Khmer nation.
In that, he tried to explain to children and some members of the public who like to use English letters and Khmer tones to use Khmer characters instead.
He expressed his condolences to some students, especially the younger generation, please pay attention to your own national literature, because if you do not pay attention and participate in promoting their own culture, literature will not be soon. It will lose everything.
Mr. Khemarak Bunna also mentioned that the use of Khmer script with the Khmer accent has been expressed by the National Literary Conservation Group, Mr. Khemarak Bunna has expressed concern, especially for children, because the use of English script with Khmer accent has flowed to children through the network. Social communication is currently widely used.
Mr. Khemarak Bunna said, "The use of the English alphabet, the Khmer accent, has a very significant impact on children and young people, because it makes children have a high rate of misspellings, and another important factor. If you continue to use the English alphabet, the Khmer accent, one day the national alphabet will be lost.
He added that the Conservatives of National Literature has made great efforts to make Cambodian children stop writing English letters in Khmer tones, and try to rewrite national literature on social media. But if they want to write in English, they can write in English, but please do not use English letters written in Khmer accents.
He added that national literature is very risky, especially for children, in fact, some children can not write national literature correctly.
Mr. Khemarak Bunna called on all relevant ministries to help monitor and correct the above issues. In addition, the National Literary Conservation Group will continue to disseminate to local people and communities, especially on this social network. Great event organized by Robotic Youth Scientific Training Center of Cambodia
