Cambodia announces multi-sectoral action plan to combat drug resistance
(Phnom Penh): On December 23, 2019, the Multidisciplinary Action Plan on Antimicrobial Resistance in Cambodia from 2019 to 2023 was announced today at Sunway Hotel, Phnom Penh by the Ministry of Health, Ministry of Agriculture and Forestry. And Fisheries and the Ministry of Environment. This is according to a press release from the Ministry of Health, which was received by Fresh News this evening.
The statement highlighted that the action plan, which sets out seven strategic priorities, is an important effort to address the threat posed by antiretroviral drugs in Cambodia. This action plan will provide a better approach than ever before, as it will increase the participation of actors from the medical and veterinary sectors, agriculture, food, finance, the environment and the general consumer.
The message of the Minister of Health attached to the action plan states, "On behalf of the relevant ministries, on behalf of the Royal Government of Cambodia, we are committed to implementing a comprehensive and coordinated approach to prevent and control drug resistance." "Viruses caused by inappropriate use of antimicrobials in medicine, food and agriculture."
Please read the details in the press release below: By 2019, there are more than 3,000 small and medium enterprises in Sihanoukville, Dr. Kun Nhim: Small and medium enterprises are the backbone of the economy and a strong driving force in Stimulating growth and creating jobs
The Governor of Kouch Chamroeun Province warmly welcomed and expressed his deep gratitude to Dr. Kun Nhim for organizing this workshop for the benefit of small and medium enterprises in Sihanoukville, which is an important economic pole in the coastal region of Cambodia. Both have good potential and favorable location, infrastructure, especially the large and busy international port and a "multi-purpose model special economic zone".
Dr. Kun Nhim emphasized that small and medium enterprises are the backbone of the economy and a strong driving force for growth and job creation. In this context, the Royal Government of Cambodia, with Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, has set out a number of measures to support the SME sector, as set out in the policy and strategic documents, including:
In the past, the Royal Government has introduced a series of measures to support the agricultural sector and small and medium enterprises, including: the reduction of customs duties, special tax rates, the imposition of value added tax on the burden of the state and the imposition of taxes. Exports are borne by the state on agricultural products, crops, livestock, pharmaceuticals, animal feed, agricultural machinery and production raw materials used in these sectors.
In order to promote economic diversification and to further promote small and medium enterprises, the Royal Government has recently provided incentives for customs and taxation through Sub-Decree No. 124 dated October 2, 2018 and Sub-Decree. No. 50, dated March 25, 2019, focusing on 8 priority areas, including:
On that occasion, Dr. Kun Nhim also stated that under the guidance of Dr. Aun Porn Moniroth, Deputy Prime Minister, Minister of Economy and Finance, the implementation of customs and tax incentives for small and medium enterprises must have a mechanism Monitor the progress of implementation as well as learn more about the suggestions and challenges of the private sector related to the implementation of this incentive.
In this workshop, professional officers of the General Department of Customs and Excise of Cambodia and representatives of the General Department of Taxation presented the contents of the Sub-Decree and Prakas of the Ministry of Economy and Finance, stating the conditions, procedures and mechanisms of provision. And monitoring customs and tax incentives for small and medium enterprises as a basis for application when intending to apply for such incentives.
