(Phnom Penh): On the morning of December 22, 2019, more than 100 landowners affected by the Phnom Penh-Sihanoukville Expressway project located in Snoul Khpos Village, Sangkat Kampol, Khan Kampol, Phnom Penh continued to gather. They protested and expressed strong dissatisfaction with the government's compensation provided to them through inter-ministerial.
Dissatisfied with the compensation, the land they bought about two years ago from a company selling a private plot of land costs $ 120 per square meter. US dollars per square meter, but received only 95 US dollars per square meter.
Recently, inter-ministerial representatives representing the Royal Government, including the Ministry of Public Works and Transport, the Ministry of Economy and Finance, the Ministry of Land Management, Urban Planning and Construction and construction contractors, told the victims that they would pay. Compensation to them is $ 95 per square meter, and if they refuse to take it, it will continue for a long time, not knowing when it will end. According to the affected people.
Speaking to reporters, Mr. Sokhom Dara claimed that compared to the current land market is expensive, so such compensation makes it impossible for them to buy land in other areas. He said that people are all borrowing money from the bank to buy land in this area, and if the government pays such inappropriate compensation, just to repay the bank is not enough.
Mr. Sokhom Dara stated: “I and other people have not benefited even a little bit on this land. So I just asked you to help bring this message to the inter-ministerial, which he set the price that I can not accept. "Let there be inspections and practical studies on how I and others can get to buy land in any other area at a reasonable market price."
Another resident, Tang Leng Chhay, said that those who came to solve the problem told them that the price of $ 95 per square meter had not changed, no matter what time it was. The inter-ministerial working group also urged the victims to get their thumbs up, claiming that if they took it now, they would get the money immediately, but if not, they would have to leave it for years to come.
When asked who set the inter-ministerial working group to determine such compensation, the working group claimed that the Royal Government was the one to do so. This is according to Mr. Taing Leng Chhay.
"The problem solver is that the state does not change the price, and no matter how long it is left, it does not go up and down, not a penny, and if you give a thumbs up, now you get money immediately, if not," he said. The next few years remain the same, unchanged. "People are trying to borrow money from the bank to buy the land and hope to build a house on it."
Mr. Taing Leng Chhay also claimed that their land had a solid title deed, not land that encroached on any location to occupy, and he hoped that the Royal Government would provide reasonable compensation for them who affected the expressway project. This is because the work of the state is done to serve the people.
Leng Thoeun, a victim who has already built a house, expressed great concern when he heard that his house would be demolished to make way for a highway, while the compensation was not appropriate. The taxi driver revealed that he had submitted a plot of land in his hometown to borrow money from a bank in order to buy land and build a house near Phnom Penh to make it easier for them to earn a living.
Another resident, Mey Taing Hun, who also suffered from land and house problems, described working hard every day to get money back from banks that bought land in the area. The woman, who works as a factory worker and has a husband who runs a tricycle, said that the news that her land had to be repaid with improper compensation was already an issue, while housing compensation was another important issue. The body does not sleep.
