Five rare species of wildlife released in the forests of Angkor
According to Mr. Yit Chanda, Deputy Director General of APSARA Authority, in the past 6 years, 5 species of rare and endangered wildlife, including 8 crowned vultures (2 are being grafted), 4 Five fragrant ants, five geckos and 12 Brahmin monkeys were bred and released to live in the Angkor Park forest under the best care.
Some of these animals came from Phnom Tamao Park and some from Koh Krapi, Koh Kong Province, and were released at three main locations in Angkor, such as the Ghost Gate, the Takao Gate, and the Ta Nei Temple, which has a safe jungle for Their lives.
Daroth said the release of wildlife in the Angkor area was intended to contribute to the conservation of wildlife that is currently endangered and endangered. In particular, wildlife is also an important source of tourist attraction because it does not harm or harm people if people do not abuse them.
For the care of these five rare species, there are two regular agents with security cameras to monitor the activities of animals and harassment of criminals. In addition, there is a direct monitoring by the Wildlife Alliance at least once a month to monitor the health of animals.
