Government Spokesperson Unit Leads a Group of Journalists to Visit Mango Plantations and Factories
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Phay Siphan, Minister Delegate to the Prime Minister and Chairman of the Royal Government Spokesperson Unit, on December 20, 2019, led a group of journalists from various institutions to visit the mango plantation and mango processing factory. Located in Kampong Speu province.
The spokesperson of the Royal Government of Cambodia, after being established in 2019, has been actively working to provide information and clarify the questions of the people, as well as show the activities and achievements of the Royal Government. . This unit, which is the messenger of the Royal Government, organizes weekly press conferences on various topics to spread awareness, understanding and activities of the Royal Government to the people.
Taking journalists to visit mango plantations and mango processing factories is another new activity of the Royal Government Spokesperson's Unit. Mr. Phay Siphan said that the organization of the journalist's visit to the mango plantation and mango processing factory in Kampong Speu to contribute to a wider dissemination of the investment potential of mango in Cambodia, especially in Kampong Speu Province.
According to the Ministry of Agriculture, there are more than 100,000 hectares of mango plantations in Cambodia. The largest mango growing areas are in Kampong Speu, Battambang, Kampot and Banteay Meanchey. Kampong Speu province is also claimed to be the province that grows the best quality mangoes, namely crispy and delicious mangoes.
Mr. Yim Nonn, Director of the Department of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, said that Kampong Speu has nearly 50,000 hectares of mango plantations, but the area harvested is nearly 40,000 hectares, equivalent to 9 million trees. In Kampong Speu province, the area under good mango cultivation is in Phnom Sruoch, Oral, Thpong and Boseth districts. According to the director of the department, Kampong Speu province has 4 factories for processing mangoes and 1 factory for packing mangoes.
During the visit to understand the cultivation and processing of mangoes in Kampong Speu province, Mr. Phay Siphan and the team from the Royal Government Spokesperson Unit as well as the media met with relevant officials in the province, such as the Director of the Department of Agriculture, Director of the Department of Commerce. Director of the Department of Industry, Director of the Department of Information and the Mango Community.
Many questions were asked by Mr. Phay Siphan and his team to the director of the department and the community to find out what the provincial authorities did to help the mango growers and what the challenges were. The province can not solve that.
Mr. Yim Nonn, Director of the Department of Agriculture, described some of the work that the Department has intervened for mango growers, such as helping to prepare varieties, training in cultivation techniques and care. However, the Department of Agriculture has trouble intervening for mango growers when they face problems due to the small number of officials in the department.
Separately, the director of the Department of Commerce mentioned some of his activities, such as going to explain to the people about the quality of mangoes, which is the definition of foreign markets. However, according to the director of the Department of Commerce, there are still some gaps and lack of responsibility in its duties to facilitate the sale of mangoes.
For the Department of Industry and Handicrafts, the director of the department did not participate, but sent his representative to participate. A representative of the director of the Department of Industry and Handicrafts said that in order to support the production of mangoes, it has done some work, especially related to the inspection of the water system that is extracted to be used for washing mangoes.
On the other hand, mango growers, although their mangoes are not currently facing market problems. However, there are still some problems such as lack of knowledge on mango cultivation, lack of labor, lack of capital, difficulty in obtaining loans from financial institutions, natural disasters and pests.
In Chhay Van, president of the Kampong Speu Mango Association, which has about 353 members, said that the current market for mangoes is better than before. But what still faces a lack of capital for mango production and labor costs.
Mr. In Chhay Van suggested that the Royal Government should prepare a budget to help the community as well as mango growers to support their activities.
In addition to meeting with officials and mango growers, Mr. Phay Siphan also brought reporters to the mango processing factory. Mr. Phay Siphan and reporters traveled to Boeung Ket Mango Candy Factory, a factory that is processing mangoes into sweets for export to the Chinese market.
Heng Sreng, a representative of the mango confectionery factory, said that the mango confectionery market was in high demand in China and the mango confectionery factory did not meet its plan.
According to Heng Sreng, his factory needs between 150 and 180 tons of mangoes a day for mango confectionery, but he only collects between 60 and 100 tons.
Heng Sreng appealed to mango growers to sell mangoes to his company, which he accepted at a reasonable price.
After meeting with professional officials representing mango growers and factories, Mr. Phay Siphan discovered some issues related to mango cultivation, marketing and processing issues. Mr. Phay Siphan emphasized that he would collect all these issues and submit a report to the Royal Government to find any suitable solution for mango growers and traders.
