Find out the procedure for removing President Donald Trump from the US Senate (Video inside) (Washington): Finally, as expected, the Democrat-controlled US House of Representatives has voted to approve the formal charges and proceedings. The ouster of Donald Trump made him the third president in US history to be ousted.
After more than six hours of debate this Wednesday (Washington time), the House of Representatives voted to approve Article 1 of the charges against Tram in a 230-vote majority against 197 votes. Article 2, which accuses him of obstructing or obstructing the parliamentary investigation, was approved by a vote of 229 to 198.
But even if the two charges are passed, it does not mean that Donald Trump's political life and US presidency are coming to an end, as both charges will be sent to the Senate for further approval. First.
What does the US Constitution say about the next procedure?
According to the US Constitution, a president who has been indicted or prosecuted by the House of Representatives can remain in office. That is, Donald Trump's presidency depends on the Senate, which is expected to hold a debate in January 2020 on whether to vote to remove Trump.
In addition, the US Constitution also states that the Senate must hold hearings, with senators acting as the judiciary, members of the House of Representatives acting as prosecutors, and the president and secretary of justice as defendants. The Senate needs a two-thirds majority to approve Donald Trump's removal as president.
Prosecution or indictment of the incumbent president for crimes is not possible, but the constitution allows for separate charges when the president is removed.
* Which presidents have been formally charged?
So far, no US president has been ousted or ousted, and Donald Trump may not be ousted either. On the other hand, according to opinion polls, the removal proceedings could also help him gain political advantage.
What do Americans think about Tram's removal?
Democrat lawmakers formally launched an investigation in October to prosecute or oust Donald Trump, accusing the president of seeking support. Overseas to defeat his rival Joe Biden in the 2020 US presidential election.
(Phnom Penh): The Anti-Technology Crime Police Department of the Ministry of Interior has arrested a man who stole fake documents related to cars (checks and credit cards) on a complaint from the Ministry of Public Works and Transport. The suspects not only forged checks and fake credit cards, but also forged official websites of the Ministry of Public Works through QR Codes.
On the morning of December 19, 2019, the Department of Technology Crime Police held a press conference to reveal the background and story of the suspect to the public after the Phnom Penh Municipal Court issued a temporary detention order on December 18, 2019 yesterday.
Lt. Gen. Siv Chhean, Deputy Director of the Technology Crime Police Department, said that the suspect was arrested on December 14, 2019 by a complaint from the Ministry of Public Works and Transport and under the direction of Lt. Gen. Dr. Neth Savoeun, National Police Commissioner General, and under the direct command of Lt. Gen. Chea Pov, Director of the Department.
Lt. Gen. Siv Chhean confirmed that according to the confession of the suspect, Bun Marady, alias Pov, 33, described that he had started to forge documents of the Ministry of Public Works, such as business license, business ID card. Vehicles (credit card), vehicle technical inspection certificate (check), annual road tax and other vehicle related documents.
The suspect also confessed that he was originally a businessman and a regular photographer, then in 2017 began to break the idea to cut documents related to the car according to the needs of customers who often tell him to do it for a fee. Higher than photography. Since 2017, it has started to make fake documents and create a fake website to store the data scanned by QR Code contained on the fake documents.
The deputy director of the department said that in addition to arresting the suspects, his professional police force also confiscated 49 fake business licenses, 84 fake credit cards, vehicle technical inspection cards. (Check) 125 sheets, as well as road tax and some other documents.
