More than 530 students and residents collect garbage on the streets in Baray and Stong districts, Kampong Thom province
By participating in World Cleanup Day in September last year, this year's NagaWorld Charity Heart has continued to raise awareness of the negative effects of disposable plastic products and how to properly manage waste.
More than 530 volunteers, including NagaWorld staff, students and community members, recently collected 492 kilograms of garbage on the streets of Baray and Stong districts in Kampong Thom province recently. Garbage is a global problem that is constantly occurring as people around the world create too much garbage.
According to the World Bank, there is an estimated 2.01 billion tons of urban solid waste every year around the world, of which at least 33% is poorly managed. Environment.
As in other developing countries, Cambodia is also facing major challenges in waste management due to population growth and urban sprawl. Public awareness of proper waste management remains limited as there are insufficient waste collection and processing plants.
According to the 2018 report of the Ministry of Environment of Cambodia, the amount of waste in the country has increased by about 10% per year. In Phnom Penh alone, about 3,000 tons of garbage is collected every day.
NagaWorld Managing Director Pern Chen said, "Garbage collection is an effective step in raising awareness of solid waste issues, which is why NagaWorld has organized a workshop on" Best Practices for Reduce plastic waste ”on August 23 and 24, 2019 in NagaWorld.
He added that we then went to collect garbage in Kampong Thom province with students and the community to create immediate change and establish good habits in proper waste management for the well-being of the community.
