(Svay Rieng): General Moeng Samphan, Secretary of State of the Ministry of National Defense and Chairman of the Cambodia-Vietnam Disaster Relief Training Commission, confirmed that the Disaster Relief Exercise in the land border area between Cambodia and Vietnam is the highest priority of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia to the people living along the border.
General Moeng Samphan continued that the border disaster was natural, because it happened without knowing and did not say in advance what happened and when. Therefore, to help the people in the border areas who have suffered from natural disasters, Samdech Techo Hun Sen has taken great care.
General Moeng Samphan said that along the Cambodian-Vietnamese border, Cambodians are affected by the effects of typhoons, which can occur every year, affecting the interests of the people and causing loss of life. Property and so on. General Moeng Samphan stated that in the world, only mobile forces can rescue people in time when people face disasters or incidents.
He said that the Royal Government of Cambodia has taken care of the people living along the border and therefore conducted disaster relief exercises using the force of the army according to the skills to be implemented and also set up a health center to treat the people. When a disaster occurs, rescue people on the border in a timely manner.
It should be informed that this is the first disaster relief exercise that Cambodia and Vietnam have done together. The Ministry of Defense will continue to do this exercise with neighboring countries such as Thailand and Laos, not just Vietnam.
Minister of Environment: Protected Area Communities Contribute to the Economic Development of the People
The Minister said that the Royal Government of Cambodia intends to turn the conservation area, which is now a natural tourism area, to meet the growing tourism sector and the needs of the people to travel. The Minister of Environment said that at present, domestic tourism has increased significantly due to the well-being of the people due to the growth of the national economy and the peace that pervades the whole country.
Cambodia is rich in natural resources and biodiversity. Forests cover about 50% of the country, the highest rate in Southeast Asia. To date, the Royal Government has increased the area of protected areas under the jurisdiction of the Ministry of Environment to about 41% of the country, equivalent to about 7.2 million hectares, making Cambodia the second largest in Asia after Bhutan.
Forests play an important role in ensuring water and energy supply, mitigating the effects of natural disasters and contributing to climate change mitigation. Ecotourism and ecosystem payment plans can also provide opportunities for the development of economic activities while supporting conservation work. As of 2019, there are about 168 communities in the protected areas of the Ministry of Environment.
Minister Say Sam Al announced to the 168 community representatives under the Ministry of Environment that the Ministry has provided environmental and social funds to support community development work that they can apply for to protect the community.
"If the community has a clear goal, the government will support the budget for community development to make our community prosperous," he said. "Apart from the peace and development that Cambodia has now, how can we help promote and enhance local community development?"
The Minister expressed the hope that with the right policy of the Royal Government of Cambodia, the community will prosper in the future. "I am confident that you will make the right decision for the future of the community and encourage the provincial authorities to work closely and coordinate with the Ministry of Environment and the community to implement community development projects."
Mr. Say Sam Al added, "The Royal Government of Cambodia, the Ministry of Environment, wants to see the smiles and solidarity of the community stronger, the children of the people study deeply, organize the community economy, grow and natural resources are better protected for "For generations to come."
