Expert: Plantation trees meet the needs of the wood processing industry and reduce the pressure on natural forest use
(Phnom Penh): The development of plantations is valuable in the supply of wood raw materials for the wood processing industry, while construction in Cambodia has been growing and increasing, especially in Phnom Penh and Sihanoukville, where building projects A lot of new things are happening. This is the statement of Mr. Lu Chu Chang, CEO of Sing Bay Otec and President of the Wood Processing Industry Association on December 14, 2019.
Mr. Lu Chuchang emphasized that since November 2019, Sing Bay Otec has started clearing 5,000 hectares and plans to start planting seedlings in May 2020 on the cleared land. All with plans to plant nearly 7 million trees. In 2021 and 2022, the company plans to clear 10,000 hectares per year and plant a total of more than 25 million seedlings.
"The plantations will bear fruit five years after planting, and the fruit will be used as a raw material for processing and processing in the country," said Luo Chuchang. There is a high demand. "Currently, due to insufficient domestic production, Cambodia also imports processed wood from abroad."
Mr. Luo Chouchang claimed that the plantations provide employment benefits for about 100,000 people in the process of planting and harvesting and processing. As in Vietnam, where they plant plantations, they export to Europe, earning billions of dollars a year and creating a lot of jobs for the people.
At the same time, the CEO of Sing Bayotech said that within a year, when the new management of Sing Bay Otec has built a road about 100 kilometers to the market to reduce the distance of transportation. Deliver and benefit the people in the area to transport their produce for sale locally or abroad.
In particular, in the project of Sing Bay Otec, the company has already built 200 kilometers of roads and plans to build hundreds of kilometers of roads. "There are labyrinthine roads in investment projects that reduce travel time and costs," said Luo Chuang.
Regarding the investment in plantations in Cambodia, Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State and spokesman for the Ministry of Environment, expressed support and said that investment in plantations is very important because it will reduce the pressure. The use of natural wood and the establishment of a wood processing industry will also help create jobs and increase income for people in the area to participate in national economic growth.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra stated that the establishment of plantations is an effective strategy to prevent natural resource crime and contribute to strengthening the conservation of natural resources that Cambodia has today. He stressed that Cambodia now has rich natural resources and biodiversity. Forests cover about 50% of the country, the highest rate in Southeast Asia.
To date, the Royal Government has increased the area of protected areas under the jurisdiction of the Ministry of Environment to about 41% of the country, equivalent to about 7.2 million hectares, making Cambodia the second largest in Asia after Bhutan.
