China suspends US tariff increase after trade agreement
BEIJING (Reuters) - Beijing has suspended tariff increases on some products imported from the United States after the two economic powers agreed on a trade deal, the State Council Customs Commission said on Sunday. Phase 1 on Friday. This was released by Reuters on Sunday, December 15, 2019.
On Friday, Chinese officials revealed that the first phase of a trade agreement negotiated between US and Chinese officials had been re-signed by both sides. In early 2020, the United States promised not to raise tariffs on Chinese goods and lift restrictions, and China promised to increase agricultural purchases from the United States by $ 50 billion, more than double the 2017 figure.
