More than 100 families have not yet resolved their land issues from the impact on the construction of the Phnom Penh-Sihanoukville Expressway, requesting the intervention of the head of the Royal Government
(Phnom Penh): While the construction of the Phnom Penh-Sihanoukville Expressway has been completed 7%, the land of more than 100 families located in Snoul Khpos Village, Sangkat Kampol, Khan Kampol, Phnom Penh. It has not been resolved yet.
Affected land among more than 100 families is a lot of land that they bought from a single company for $ 4,000 to $ 20,000 with a valid title deed issued by the authorities. Also. Remarkably, some families applied for a plot of land in their hometown to borrow money from the bank to buy the plot, hoping to build a house near Phnom Penh to make a living.
After buying the land, as the land is part of the expressway project, the local authorities told them not to build houses, but to wait for the authorities to settle and reimburse them in order to buy land elsewhere. However, the victims claim that they have not yet received compensation.
Speaking to the media on December 15, 2019, a group of people who have not yet received compensation urgently requested Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia "Please intervene to solve, because they today have "Some are renting their houses for temporary accommodation." They also requested the government to "reimburse the market price of land so that they can use that compensation to buy land elsewhere."
The victim, Tang Leng Chhay, said, "The land survey and measurement have been completed, but the compensation is long, so I want the Royal Government and Samdech Techo Hun Sen to intervene to speed up the payment because the price of land has risen and people are buying." Some people take the land from the bank. Renting a house and paying for a plot of land is very difficult to run, so living is very difficult because you can not build a house.
Another resident, Rano, expressed deep despair over the compensation that local authorities had claimed.
