(Kampot): Mr. Chiao Tay, Governor of Kampot Province, on December 14, 2019, led the relevant professional officers and departments around the province and local authorities to inspect the dry rice fields. In fact, the farmers have intervened to pump water to dry rice fields to save rice for the people in Angkunh village, Tnaot Chong Srong commune, Banteay Meas district, Kampot province, with an area of more than 500 hectares.
According to the report of the Governor of Banteay Meas District, Mr. Muong Noeun, on that occasion, said that the dry rice fields that are facing damage due to lack of water in 3 villages in Tnaot Chong Srong commune on a total cultivated area of about 512 hectares.
So far, the dry rice crop has not been damaged yet, but because the people can not afford to pump water to the rice fields, they asked the provincial administration, as well as professional officials and stakeholders to provide emergency intervention.
During the meeting with the people, Mr. Chiao Tai said that taking into account the well-being of the people is the most important thing for the provincial leaders, and today's visit is not just to pump water to save rice for the people. Not only that, he wants to help solve problems and care for the local people more.
The provincial governor also addressed the requests of local authorities and people with three main issues, including:
* First: to help pump water to dry rice to save rice in time * Second: to find a more expensive market for rice, the President of the Provincial Chamber of Commerce must do his best to attract investors or large rice traders More * Third: The Provincial Department of Water Resources and Meteorology must study the canal system clearly, whether to restore at the request of the people or not.
However, the governor of Chiao Tai province has asked people to produce dry rice or rice (ER) only twice this year, because the Vietnamese river that flows to irrigate Banteay Meas district has decreased. Extremely strong, while forecasts that Cambodia may face a prolonged drought.
Doctors Reveal Causes, Prevention and Treatment as 400 Million People Worldwide Suffer From Rhinitis
The meeting focused on a variety of topics from well-known speakers from countries such as Spain, Brazil, the United States and Canada through live broadcasts from Lyon, France.
Topics discussed at the meeting included the prevalence of nasal allergies, the function of air pollution in the severity of nasal allergies, the management of the treatment of nasal allergies exacerbated by nasal congestion. Atmosphere.
Currently, about 400 million people worldwide suffer from nasal allergies. By 2050, allergies are expected to affect one in two people, or 50% of the world.
The International Joint Statement on "Management of Nasal Allergies Caused by Severe Pollution", recognized by the World Health Organization (WAO), was also released in During the meeting.
Dr. Hing Kim, medical manager at Sanofi Cambodia, explains, “Nasal allergies occur when someone's immune system reacts to an allergen, such as pollen, pet dander, feces, small insects, fungi In the environment, it causes inflammation of the lining of the nose.
Common symptoms include nasal congestion, runny nose, itching or watery eyes, sneezing, itchy nose and throat. Pollution can make these symptoms worse.
Since its inception in 1951 in Zurich, the conference has attracted more than 4,000 professionals and scientists working and interested in allergies, immunity and other related areas each year to change. Suggest and share new knowledge. In particular, in 2019, Sanofi is also a supporter of this meeting.
Every year, Sanofi Pharmaceuticals Cambodia always pays attention and actively contributes to raising awareness, sharing new scientific knowledge to health care professionals and participating in many other social activities. . Another new international hospital to open early next year
