Army: Candidates for the Civil Service Examination will be expelled
(Phnom Penh): Lt. Gen. Bun Sopheap, Permanent Deputy Chairman of the Examination Commission, confirmed that for the officer recruitment exam in 2020, any candidate who violates the internal order of the Examination Commission will be expelled from the exam by No more exceptions required.
Lt. Gen. Bun Sopheap's warning was issued on December 13, 2019, when he was invited to represent Lt. Gen. Hun Manet, Deputy Commander-in-Chief, Commander-in-Chief of the Army and Deputy Chief of Staff. Examination committee in the ceremony to summarize the results of the middle level working group of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces (RCAF) 2020 recruitment competition at the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces Command.
"As long as they are caught, they will not be able to comply with the internal regulations, so they will be expelled from the exam because more than 8,000 candidates took the exam this time, we only took 620 people," he said. It is not difficult to choose a good and capable officer to do that.
Lt. Gen. Bun Sopheap stated that such restrictions are to recruit officers who have the potential and sufficient capacity to serve as officers to take part in enhancing the prestige of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces.
Lt. Gen. But Sopheap called on the committee to correct all candidates' scores to correct the candidates' documents on the actual ability of all candidates, because in the past there was negligence on the candidates' scores.
In a reaffirmation of Germany's long-standing partnership with the Mekong River Commission, the Fund aims to promote dialogue and cooperation on cross-border water resources planning and management among the countries along the Lower Mekong River. There are Cambodia, Laos, Thailand and Vietnam.
For a period of three years (2019-2021), this grant aims to enhance cross-border environmental impact monitoring efforts from completed dams along the Mekong River, including the Xayaburi Dam, including the Xayaburi Dam. And Don Sahong will provide measures to mitigate the impact through the MRC Joint Environmental Monitoring Program.
"Germany's support comes at a critical time as the Mekong River is facing a difficult balance between development, energy, transport and agriculture, and environmental and environmental impacts," said Dr. Hatda. Livelihood of local people.
According to the latest MRC water flow monitoring results, the 2019 drought has caused the Mekong River to fall to its lowest level in history or during the last 60 years. Most areas along the river basin have been experiencing remarkably low water flow since June.
The results further suggest that extremely low water flow, slow depletion of sediments and the presence of algae or sand on the sand and riverbed are the reasons for the recent blue-green color of the Mekong River.
The MRC has warned that blue-green water conditions could occur elsewhere and pose risks such as changes in river productivity, reduced biodiversity in water caused by clear water. .
Dr. Hatta added, "These emerging issues require detailed and prompt monitoring and reporting for proper river basin management." "I would like to thank Germany for making this possible."
Germany has provided nearly 50 million euros in technical and financial support to the MRC since 1995. These German supports enable the MRC to carry out various strategic activities, including institutional reform, sustainable hydropower development, integrated water resources management, flood mitigation and climate change.
