Suu Kyi warns that "Rohingya crisis will erupt again" if the international community does not drop the charges against Myanmar ...
The Hague: Nobel Peace Prize winner and Myanmar State Councilor Aung San Suu Kyi has urged a United Nations (UN) tribunal judge to drop her country's indictment on genocide. Indigenous Rohingya Muslims. But she also warned that the Rohingya crisis could erupt again if the case continues. This was released by CNA on Friday morning, December 13, 2019.
Aung San Suu Kyi's warning came during her final testimony on Thursday, following a three-day hearing at the ICJ in The Hague, the Netherlands. "Allowing the Gambia's anti-genocide lawsuit against Myanmar to proceed further could lead to a resurgence of the Rohingya conflict as well as damage to national reconciliation," he said.
Phnom Penh Governor Announces New Plan to Reduce the Use of Sweet Drinks in Schools for Urban Well-Being
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Khuong Sreng, Governor of Phnom Penh, on December 13, 2019, announced that the Phnom Penh Capital Administration is implementing a reduction in the consumption of sweet drinks in schools for the welfare of the city to ensure longevity.
The announcement of the new plan is based on the Urban Wellness Partnership, a world-renowned network of 70 cities committed to saving lives by preventing non-communicable diseases (such as cancer or diabetes) and injuries. This is according to the press release of Phnom Penh Capital Administration, which was received by Fresh News this morning, December 13.
The Urban Wellness Partnership is expanding from 54 cities to 70 cities, with more than 300 million people living around the world.
Mr. Khuong Sreng emphasized that improving public health requires strong action, reaffirming his joint commitment to partnership for urban well-being after organizing outreach workshops and outreach campaigns. Drink sweet drinks at the end of 2018 and the beginning of 2019.
"We are proud to be part of a prominent group of cities working to ensure the longevity and full time of our citizens," he said. Through the Urban Well-Being Partnership, our efforts in this area have yielded great positive results. "We are implementing a global plan for effective urban welfare."
In Phnom Penh, the consumption of sweet drinks is a problem. In 2013, the World Health Organization (WHO) school survey found that 52% of urban youth consumed at least one can of sweetened beverages a day, and more than 60% saw advertisements for sweetened or ready-to-eat drinks. When watching TV or playing video games.
Mr. Khuong Sreng vowed to continue to raise awareness of the effects of excessive consumption of sugary drinks and to reduce the consumption of sugary drinks in schools by strengthening the implementation of Guideline No. 18 AEC, dated Thursday Kor Eksak BE 2562 is on May 2, 2019 of the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports.
Of the 10 deaths worldwide, eight are due to non-communicable diseases and injuries. In low-income and middle-income cities, the value added per capita to implement a key policy to prevent non-communicable diseases between now and 2030 is only $ 1 and 27 cents when the cost of an accident is equal to 3% of GDP in many countries.
Through this partnership, many cities are committed to implementing one of 14 interventions that address the risk factors for non-communicable diseases and injuries, such as enforcing smoke-free laws that protect citizens from inhaling secondhand smoke and restricting the promotion of sweetened beverages. And junk food or create a lane for cyclists.
