This information was confirmed by Deputy Governor of Kampong Chhnang Province, Mr. Sun Sovannarith in a press conference organized by the Spokesperson of the Royal Government of Cambodia this morning, December 12, 2019.
The deputy governor also said that about 4,563 families had been relocated. There are 2,379 Vietnamese immigrant families, equivalent to 10,087 people, in addition to Khmer and Khmer-Muslim citizens. Mr. Sovannarith also pointed out that the relocation work here did not have any intervention from the Vietnamese authorities or government, that is, Cambodia has organized according to its plans and work.
Vietnamese immigrants, as well as Cambodian and Khmer-Muslim people who came to live on the banks of the Tonle Sap, are claimed to have settled here after 1979 after Cambodia was liberated from the genocidal regime of Pol Pot. . Regarding Vietnamese immigrants, some Cambodians also questioned why the Cambodian authorities did not send these immigrants back to their home countries.
Mr. Sun Sovannarith explained that although the Vietnamese immigrants were not Cambodians, they were protected by international human rights law and that Cambodia, a member of the United Nations, must participate in enforcing the law. Human rights issues are also covered by international law. That is, Cambodia cannot force any nation in Cambodia to return to their country in violation of international law.
Although Cambodia did not deport Vietnamese immigrants living on the Tonle Sap, some Vietnamese migrants, fearing hardship when moving to the mainland, volunteered to return to their home countries. . This is according to Mr. Sun Sovannarith.
Concerned about the relocation, there are also fears that these Vietnamese immigrants will be granted citizenship in exchange for moving to land instead. Answering this question, the Deputy Governor of Kampong Chhnang Province confirmed that living on water or on land is all Khmer territory and immigrants are still immigrants without citizenship.
Mr. Sun Sovannarith also stated that currently, out of a total of 3,851 houses and other businesses, both on the banks and on the surface of the Tonle Sap, for the houses without fish, 100 were selected on the mainland. Percentage, but has not yet reached the 40-hectare site that the authorities have prepared. As many as 750 fish in six targets were not relocated, but authorities were required to remove walls and bathrooms.
The deputy governor of Kampong Chhnang province also claimed that the 40 hectares of land can be purchased by Cambodians and Khmer Muslims as private property, but for Vietnamese immigrants can not buy, only the right to rent to stay.
For the 40-hectare site, authorities are speeding up land reclamation and setting up infrastructure to allow migrants to build homes. Currently, only about 100 people live on the land.
