Family Helmet Program Provides Helmets to Students in Battambang Primary School
(Battambang): The “Helmet for Family” program, which is being implemented by the Asian Injury Prevention Foundation with the support of Manulife Cambodia, provided 1,238 helmets to students, teachers and parents at Anlong Vil Primary School. Located in Anlong Vil commune, Sangke district, Battambang province.
The ceremony was held on the afternoon of December 12, 2019 with the participation of provincial and national representatives as well as relevant officials in Battambang, as well as stakeholders on traffic safety, as well as management - staff of Manulife Cambodia and organizations. Asian Injury Prevention Foundation as well.
Mrs. Mon Manvi, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Public Works and Transport and Secretary General of the National Road Safety Committee, expressed her pleasure to see the implementation of this program in Battambang Province, as the province also has a high accident rate. Among all the capital provinces of Cambodia.
Therefore, the team must provide appropriate protection for children to raise awareness to promote safe travel to and from school. She also called on all children to wear helmets when traveling by bicycle or motorbike, whether riding near or far, and to remind parents and guardians to protect their lives and fitness and property from traffic accidents.
The Family Helmet Program, launched in Cambodia in 2012, aims to provide quality helmets to students, teachers and parents, as well as to promote helmet use through parental involvement. Model to children and provide opportunities for training that focuses on mothers as well as traffic safety behaviors of their children.
Kim Panha, director of the Asian Injury Prevention Foundation in Cambodia, claims that children see their parents as role models and decision makers, but have a low rate of parents who remind their children to wear helmets when riding a motorcycle. Therefore, parents and guardians should encourage and encourage their children to follow the road traffic law consistently (wearing a helmet) as they want their children to listen and follow their advice and discipline.
At the same time, he requested the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports to set a helmet as an internal regulation for students when traveling to school, because the law stipulates that children from the age of 3 and travel by motorcycle ( School) must wear a helmet. Students who are the vines or future human resources of the nation should be the ones who participate in the implementation and implementation of the law and should start from the law on road traffic first.
