(Phnom Penh): Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng, Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of the Ministry of Interior and Chairman of the National Committee for Combating Human Trafficking, on behalf of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, presides over the celebration on December 12, 2019. The nation fights human trafficking into its 13th year.
The ceremony was held at the Koh Pich Theater with the participation of leaders of all institutions, the Senate, the National Assembly, the Royal Government, diplomats, representatives of residences in Cambodia, national and international guests, government officials, the Armed Forces, teachers, students, the Union of Youth Federations. Cambodia Youth Scouts and CRC Youth Employers, Workers, Monks, Religious Representatives, Grandmother Tachi and more than 3,000 people.
The event will continue to be held in the provinces after the national ceremonies in Phnom Penh are over, demonstrating the Royal Government of Cambodia's commitment at both the national and sub-national levels to combat human trafficking.
This year's National Day aims to disseminate and present the message to the public, national and international organizations, the private sector, civil servants in all ministries, institutions, the armed forces of all kinds, as well as priests of all religious denominations throughout Cambodia in the sense of understanding and Collaborate to prevent and suppress all forms of crime related to human trafficking in Cambodia.
Human trafficking is recognized around the world as an intolerable crime and a serious violation of human rights under national and international law.
Crimes involving all forms of human trafficking are more complex than general criminal offenses, including manipulation, action, means, and exploitation, which require high awareness and cooperation, both domestically and internationally, without admission. The responsibility of any institution or country alone.
