(Phnom Penh): The spokesperson of the Royal Government of Cambodia this morning, December 12, 2019, held a press conference to present the plan to relocate people, including Vietnamese, Khmer and Khmer-Muslims, from living on floating houses. Tonle Sap came to live on an area of about 40 hectares in Kampong Chhnang province.
The purpose of this press conference is to explain and answer the questions of the public regarding the relocation of people living on floating houses in 9 communes / sangkats who have lived on the surface of the Tonle Sap River to return to live on land by the authorities. Kampong Chhnang Province.
Please note that this press conference was held after a reaction from some Cambodians. The Cambodian people who reacted expressed concern that giving land to Vietnamese people would lead to the loss of land in Cambodia, while others misunderstood that giving land to Vietnamese people would cut off land.
Kampong Chhnang provincial authorities have been preparing Khmer people, Khmer Muslims and Vietnamese immigrants, as well as other businesses, a total of 3,851 houses, equivalent to 4,563 families living on the Tonle Sap River, a type of floating house in the area. 9 communes / sangkats to live on land instead. Authorities have prepared about 40 hectares of land in Dambok Kak village, Svay Chrum commune, Rolea Bier district for these people to live.
Authorities stated that the Kampong Chhnang Provincial Administration has also received recommendations from the Royal Government and the Ministry of Interior to implement this plan strictly, timely and cautious in accordance with Cambodian and international law, as well as efforts to address administrative services in A new place for those people to be good.
When the provincial administration began implementing the project, Rona Smith, the UN Special Rapporteur on Human Rights in Cambodia, and her team came to monitor and monitor. Especially related to the protection of human rights.
Ms. Rona Smith stressed that the provincial administration should take care to organize this work well, such as infrastructure, clean water, electricity, schools, health, security and respect for traditional customs.
Authorities also claimed that in order to live on the 40-hectare site, Cambodians or Khmer Muslims had to buy land provided by the state at a reasonable price. Separately, Vietnamese immigrants must be hired in accordance with Cambodian immigration law.
Kampong Chhnang provincial authorities have also confirmed that the provincial administration will no longer allow residents to set up permanent residences or establish businesses in floating homes. Only fish are allowed to be raised without permanent residence, but must be prepared according to the techniques of the Department of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries.
Kampong Chhnang Provincial Administration has also expressed its firm position that it will strictly promote and plan the relocation of floating houses until it is completed. Kampong Chhnang Provincial Administration also called on the people and relevant institutions to join the provincial administration to implement this project to achieve good results to contribute to the protection of the Tonle Sap, water environment, biodiversity and natural resources for future generations.
Minister of Commerce encourages Japanese investors to invest in Cambodia
(Phnom Penh): Minister of Commerce Pan Sosak encouraged and called on Japanese investors to invest in Cambodia for profit. To attract the attention of Japanese investors, Minister Pan Sosak highlighted the many opportunities and potentials of Cambodia that are highly favorable for investment.
The minister encouraged Japanese investors to invest more in food processing and agriculture, ecotourism, high-value manufacturing and vocational education and training, which will bring mutual benefits for the people of the country. Both.
Mr. Pan Sosak emphasized to the Japan-Cambodia Association as well as the Japanese investors that investing in Cambodia does not only serve the Cambodian market. But serve Asia, Europe, the United States and other markets through Cambodia's integration into those markets through future multilateral and bilateral trade agreements.
The Minister also expressed his pleasure at the investment of Japanese companies, one of the leading investors in Cambodia.
In response to Minister of Commerce Pan Sosak, Takahashi Fumiaki said that the Japan-Cambodia Association will continue to promote Cambodia to more members to better understand Cambodia and attract small and medium enterprises. Japan with high technology to invest and transfer knowledge to Cambodia.
In addition, representatives of some companies who attended the meeting presented projects that they plan to invest in, such as energy, silk and cosmetics made from her worms and railway infrastructure.
Please note that Japan is a major trading partner of Cambodia. In 2018, the trade volume of the two countries will be more than 1.588 million US dollars. Cambodia's exports to Japan amounted to about 1.079 million US dollars. Cambodia imports about 509 million US dollars from Japan.
As for investment, from 1994 to the first quarter of 2019, the Council for the Development of Cambodia registered 137 investment projects of Japanese companies with a total investment of about 2.5 billion US dollars. The investment focuses on the production of electrical and electronic components, auto parts and technical equipment, agro-industry, food processing, hotels, tourism, hospitals and supermarkets.
In addition to the investment projects registered with the Council for the Development of Cambodia above, there is the presence of Japanese business activities, both small and large, about 1,700 companies active in various sectors such as banking, aviation and restaurants. In the banking sector, there are currently six Japanese-owned banks operating in Cambodia.
