Joint UN program extends aid to Cambodia for another 4 years, with plans to spend more than $ 8 million
(Siem Reap): The Board of Directors of the United Nations Joint Program "Together for Youth Careers and Employment in Cambodia" plans to launch the second phase of the "Youth Empowerment Project in Cambodia" for the next four years. (2020-2023), with plans to spend more than $ 8 million from the Royal Government, the Swiss Agency for Development and Cooperation (SDC), the United Nations and other private partners. .
This statement was made at the 5th Joint Meeting of the United Nations Joint Program Committee, co-chaired and coordinated by Heng Sour, Secretary of State, Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training, and Pauline Tamesis, UN Coordinator. National in Cambodia, held from December 9-10, 2019 at Soma Devi Angkor Resort and Spa.
The purpose of this meeting is to review the achievements of the program in the first phase, discuss and approve the second phase and action plan for 2020, and launch the implementation of the second phase, which will last for four years. From 2020-2023.
It should be reminded that the first phase of this joint project was implemented in September 2017 and will be successfully completed in November 2019 for a period of 2 years with a total budget of 4,478,000 USD. Charitable support and strategic involvement from the Swiss Agency for Development and Cooperation (SDC) and the United Nations in partnership with the Royal Government of Cambodia.
This first step contributes to facilitating the entry of young people into the labor market and reduces inequality, especially for disadvantaged and vulnerable groups, and ensures that young women and youth are more capable of responding to market needs. The work has gotten better.
The second phase, which is scheduled to be implemented in 2020, is a four-year period (2020-2023) called "Decent Work for Youth in Cambodia" and is expected to cost more than $ 8 million. Contribution from the Royal Government, the Swiss Agency for Development and Cooperation (SDC), participating agencies, the United Nations and other private partners.
The second phase is expected to continue to contribute to more opportunities for young men and women to get decent and productive jobs to achieve the 2030 development agenda, especially the eighth goal "to promote economic growth. Productivity, decent work, sustainable and environment for all "in line with the vision of Cambodia 2030-2050 and the framework for the goals of sustainable development in Cambodia 2016-2030.
The meeting also approved the executive principles of the Board of Directors, the Advisory Council and the Technical Review Committee. Approve and launch the second phase of the joint program for a period of 4 years from January 2020-2023.
