(Phnom Penh): Mr. Chea Chanto, Governor of the National Bank of Cambodia, thanked the International Monetary Fund (IMF) for continuing to provide technical assistance to the National Bank of Cambodia in developing the banking sector to be as prosperous and strong as it is now.
The Governor welcomed and thanked the IMF for continuing to provide technical assistance to the National Bank of Cambodia in developing the banking sector to grow and be strong as it is now.
He said the IMF was paying close attention to the financial crisis after the global financial crisis because it was important for the financial sector and the economy as a whole.
Please be informed that Mr. Clemens Bonner will start his mission in early 2020 to further strengthen the skills of the National Bank of Cambodia officials in monitoring, analyzing and implementing careful macro policies effectively. Professional officers cooperate with the private sector to disseminate knowledge to the people of Kampong Speu province about pesticides on mango crops
(Kampong Speu): Kampong Speu provincial officials and stakeholders are now trying to educate the people in this province to produce good mango agricultural products that can be exported to foreign markets, especially China.
Mr. Chhay Pich, Deputy Director of the Department of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries of Kampong Speu Province, said that the workshop was very beneficial in response to the Royal Government's ambitions to increase the average income of the people by 2030.
He added that Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries Veng Sakhon recently signed a memorandum of understanding with China to export mangoes. Therefore, this workshop is a good advantage for the people who make them have the tradition of growing mangoes in the traditional way, but also understand the cultivation of techniques and use of pesticides in a technical way. Make good mangoes can be exported.
One villager who attended the workshop said he owned up to 15 hectares of mango plantations. He complained that in the past, he always faced two challenges: first, the production of mangoes did not respond to the market due to pests, and secondly, when the mangoes were harvested, there was no market.
Speaking at the seminar, Mr. Pham Van Loi, Director of Kateva Cambodia and Vietnam, said that mango is a potential agricultural product for export from Cambodia to China, Korea, Japan, Europe and ASEAN member countries. About 70,000 hectares of mango plantations have a production capacity of 8 million tons for export, earning about $ 400 million a year.
Please note that the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries has called on all mango plantation owners in Cambodia who wish to export mangoes to China and other countries, please hurry to register with the General Department of Agriculture. Professional officers to evaluate and guide the technical characteristics in accordance with export standards.
Mr. Ngin Chhay, Royal Government Delegate in charge of the General Department of Agriculture of the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, instructed all mango growers and farmers to cooperate with the General Department of Agriculture as well as the Provincial Department of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries to implement. Strict technical rules, otherwise the mango plantations of farmers can not register for export to China and other countries.
Please be informed that Cambodian mangoes are currently exported to Vietnam, Thailand, Singapore, France and Russia. In the first 11 months of 2019, Cambodia exported nearly 50,000 tons of mangoes.
