(Sihanoukville): Five streams and canals in Sihanoukville, the main drainage system from the flood to the sewage treatment plant, were started by the authorities to dig deeper and widen to ensure Drainage avoids flooding during the rainy season. The excavation of the canals and streams will cost about 10 billion riels and is scheduled for completion in March 2020.
Senior Minister Sun Chanthol, Minister of Public Works and Transport, and Mr. Kouch Chamroeun, Governor of Preah Sihanouk Province, jointly announced the groundbreaking ceremony for the rehabilitation of 5 streams and canals on the morning of December 9, 2019.
At the groundbreaking ceremony this morning, the Senior Minister highlighted the government's efforts to prioritize the restoration of the five natural streams and canals and existing sewers that are being encroached upon. Water and flood control, construction of wastewater treatment plants and expansion of sewerage and other physical infrastructure needs.
In 2018, the amount of wastewater discharged in Sihanoukville will reach 25,000 cubic meters per day and is projected to increase to 47,000 cubic meters per day by 2023 and may continue to increase to 64,000 000 cubic meters per day by 2030. The Royal Government is considering measures to address infrastructure in Sihanoukville, including drainage and wastewater storage in Sihanoukville.
During the rainy season of 2019, Sihanoukville was also severely flooded by rain. Therefore, the operation of rehabilitating the five streams and canals is an important contribution to avoiding flooding in Sihanoukville during the rainy season, as the population of various buildings is increasing.
The Senior Minister reiterated the recommendation of the Prime Minister of Cambodia, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, to allow the use of the Royal Government's savings. In particular, the principle of "take palm leaves, pack palm sugar" must be applied in the construction and repair of roads in the city, bypasses, rainwater harvesting and sewage systems in Sihanoukville.
On that occasion, the Senior Minister mentioned that the expansion of the deep-sea port can make it easier to transport large ships in and out, make transportation costs cheaper, and promote the construction and expansion of international airports to enable large aircraft. Easy parking to provide more convenient air services and the project to build an expressway connecting Phnom Penh to Sihanoukville, which is scheduled to open at the end of 2022.
