BEIJING (Reuters) - The East Turkey Islamic Movement is a terrorist organization judged by the United Nations Security Council. For a long time, the "Eastern Turkish Islamic Movement" has instigated a series of terrorist attacks to reach the goal of separating the Xinjiang region from China.
The organization also plans terrorist activities outside China, posing a serious threat to the security and stability of other countries. A CGTN documentary titled "Black Hands Behind - East Turkish Islamic Movement and Terrorism in Xinjiang" Reveals the Black Hands Behind the Black Hands.
The Xinjiang region is a "major battlefield" for China in the face of the escalation of terrorism and the fight against terrorism. Throughout the Autonomous Region, terrorist incidents have occurred frequently, posing a serious threat to the security, lives and property of the people. Incomplete statistics show that from 1990 to the end of 2016, there were thousands of terrorist attacks in Xinjiang.
On June 29, 2012, on flight G57554 en route from Hetian to Ulumuchi, there was a terrorist incident, an attempted hijacking. On April 30, 2014, a suicide bomber struck with a knife and a suicide bomber at a train station south of Urumqi, killing three people and wounding 79 others.
The "Eastern Turkish Islamic Movement" used religion to spread extremist ideology, incite hatred and separatism. The movement has spread videos about terrorism and violence by using these videos to increase membership or inculcate their extremist ideology. The organization also trains women and children to take part in terrorist attacks.
For the past 20 years, terrorists in Xinjiang have used religious extremism as an excuse to create terrorist attacks in order to divide China. Not only that, international terrorist groups continue to cross the border into Chinese territory to escalate terrorist attacks through conflicts of religion, civilization and nation.
Western media have confirmed that tensions in Xinjiang are due to economic and cultural factors. These Western media outlets focused on ethnic, religious, and cultural issues, but did not look at the history of Xinjiang.
Professor Lisheng, a former director of the Center for Border Studies at the Chinese Academy of Social Sciences, said China has made remarkable achievements in the fight against terrorism by using all means to prevent terrorist crimes. He said counter-terrorism needed to eradicate terrorism from an ideological point of view and required long-term efforts.
