Senior officials of the Ministry of Agriculture call on relevant ministries, institutions, development partners to cooperate with the Forestry Administration to strengthen law enforcement and forest management with a high sense of responsibility
The senior official's call was made at the opening of the 6th annual meeting of the National Community Forestry Coordinating Committee in Siem Reap Angkor.
He said that the Royal Government of Cambodia, under the wise and firm leadership of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister of Cambodia, considers the sustainable management and development of the forest sector as a priority political agenda to sustain people's livelihoods. People reduce the poverty of rural communities, as well as maintain the balance of biodiversity in nature, and especially contribute to tackling climate change as well as global warming in this situation.
He said that the National Community Forestry Program Coordinating Committee (NPCC) was established in 2007, headed by the Community Forestry Office, with the participation of partner organizations at the national level. This is a coordination mechanism with local community development partners and stakeholders, as well as finding strategies to support effective community forestry development in Cambodia.
He added that through the National Community Forestry Program Coordinating Committee, the Forestry Administration will strive to strengthen cooperation with development partners and stakeholders to ensure effective community forest development, including forest land management, forest management, use of non-timber forest products. And increasing the income of forest community members.
During the meeting, Mr. Ung Sam Ath, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Agriculture, urged the meeting to consider some key points, which are the following recommendations:
First, to promote and increase agricultural productivity through the intensification and diversification of agriculture in crops, animal husbandry, fish farming, agro-forestry and non-timber forest products that serve the food and nutrition sector, as well as promote private investment and increase employment. Basically.
Second, strengthen and expand community forestry and self-help groups such as savings groups, rice banks, animal banks, credit groups, etc. in both quality and quantity.
Third, to promote and implement options to improve community livelihoods in community forests by increasing education, training and dissemination of new technologies to increase agricultural productivity, processing non-timber forest products, which contributes to reducing poverty. And increase the national economy.
Fourth, to encourage people to plant trees in the fields, plantations around houses, in public places, which will be able to provide wood for construction, firewood, charcoal, etc. In particular, help increase soil fertility.
Fifth, strengthen mechanisms to monitor and prevent forest crime and illegal logging in all forms.
Sixth, call on donors, development partners, national and international NGOs, local authorities at all levels, relevant authorities to continue to cooperate as much as possible to positively contribute to the reduction of food insecurity and poverty alleviation. Rural.
