This rare and historic semi-final match has received a lot of attention from Cambodian fans. Senior official of the Ministry of Social Affairs: Visit to Thailand focuses on helping victims of human trafficking successfully
(Phnom Penh): The visit to Thailand focusing on helping victims of human trafficking ended with great success.
Great success because the Thai side agreed to Cambodia's request, the Thai side will equip all the facilities in the center, including training of professional staff as well as staff in the center, and the Thai side will add its own experts to accompany In managing the center so that the center operates sustainably.
This was stated by Mr. Nhek Vannara, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Social Affairs, Veterans and Youth Rehabilitation, representing Mr. Vong Soth, Minister of Social Affairs, Veterans and Youth Rehabilitation, to the press at Phnom Penh International Airport on the night of December 6, 2019 about the results of the visit. Of the Inter-Ministerial Delegation in Thailand from December 2 to 6.
Mr. Nhek Vannara said that as planned, the visit discussed a number of points, including:
* First: the exchange of information from victims of human trafficking for the purpose of repatriation and integration * Second: the facilitation of repatriation and integration * Third: Coordination in efforts to provide legal assistance to Victims of human trafficking * Fourth: Monitoring and estimating the results of repatriation and integration.
It should be noted that Cambodia and Thailand have agreed to build a stop center in Poipet, which will start construction in early 2020 to rescue and provide services to victims and victims of human trafficking at a cost of about 1 Million US dollars as a grant from the Royal Government of Thailand for the friendship between the two countries.
Mr. Nhek Vannara added that the decision to build the first and first shelter for victims of human trafficking in Poipet City now shows the positive starting point of Cambodia-Thailand on strengthening and managing cases. Repatriation and integration of victims of human trafficking in Thailand, signed by Cambodia and Thailand on 22 April 2019.
He said that the establishment of shelters to help victims at the border in many places along the border is very important and has contributed to social work to take responsibility for the vulnerable better and more effectively. More than before, especially to raise the profile of the Royal Government and Cambodia.
According to a report from the Department of Anti-Human Trafficking and Victim Integration, the department has so far received a total of 117 repatriation victims from around the world, including 57 women victims. The victims were from Malaysia (41), Indonesia (20), Thailand (45), China (4), Japan (6) and Singapore (1).
