The following is the report of the Ministry of Public Works and Transport: Finished! More South Korean women no longer want a husband and want to be single for life, according to a survey
(Seoul): I am an ordinary woman who is no longer interested in having a romantic relationship or marrying a man, this is the words of a South Korean woman who is determined not to marry until her death while in Korean society. Gemstones are surveyed and found that more and more women do not want to marry or have children. This is according to a quote published by AFP on Friday evening, December 6, 2019.
In fact, according to opinion polls in South Korea more than a decade ago, nearly 47 percent of single women said they considered marriage "important." But in 2018, that number dropped to 22.4 percent, while the number of married couples dropped to 257,600 from 434,900 in 1996.
The exact cause of the decline is not yet clear, but South Korea is known as a conservative and patriarchal society, or male-dominated, often female. Some wives have many important responsibilities in the family, including raising children, earning money, and caring for relatives.
