(Siem Reap): International boxer and martial artist Chan Ratana has become a representative for a new campaign designed to change men's attitudes from violence against women, especially in families, which is called " Virtue fighter.
The Virtue Fighters Campaign was launched by Life in Cambodia, an organization in Siem Reap that has already won five international awards for its anti-violence campaign. The campaign gained popularity with the production of the video, which was shared 10,000 times and viewed more than 1 million times, with hundreds of positive messages from Facebook users.
The Virtue Fighters Campaign is part of the "16 Days Campaign Against Gender Violence", inspired by an old Khmer proverb, "Khmer children have resin blood as fighters", reflecting the great influence of the Angkorian era. . Unfortunately, one in three Cambodian men misused their force and admitted to using physical or sexual violence against their partner at least once in their lives.
This campaign wants to recall the virtues of Angkorian warriors. True virtuous fighters are those who are determined to protect women and children, not to harm them.
Mr. Chan Ratana said that he was very proud to participate in this important campaign. He learned Khmer martial arts from a young age and it taught him not to use violence outside the ring and to show respect to others, especially women.
However, this campaign does not emphasize awareness alone. The Cambodian public is also asked to take a number of steps to support the campaign.
Life in Cambodia calls on the Cambodian people to be committed to being a virtuous fighter by making three promises: 1: Never use violence against vulnerable people, 2: Respect everyone, especially women, 3 : Teach the next generation to adhere to the same values.
