Source: CNN (Thursday, December 05, 2019) The University of Cambodia offers 100% scholarships for 250 second-degree undergraduates
(Phnom Penh): A special opportunity has arrived for students all over Cambodia who wish to apply for a 100% full scholarship exam from a Cambodian university.
The University of Cambodia is now offering 100% full scholarships for 250 Bachelor's Degrees (BA) to students for the second semester in March 2020.
The purpose of this scholarship is to contribute to the promotion of education and human resource development in Cambodia, which the Royal Government has identified as the first priority area, as well as to contribute to the national development and further integration of Cambodia into the region, as well as Into the international community.
The Royal Government of Cambodia has sought to find more overseas markets for mango exports. According to Ngin Chhay, the Royal Government of Cambodia's delegate in charge of the Director General of the General Department of Agriculture, Thailand, Vietnam, Hong Kong, France and Russia are currently Cambodia's fresh mango markets.
The Royal Government of Cambodia has also opened talks with the Chinese and South Korean governments to further expand fresh mango exports.
In a press conference organized by the Spokesperson of the Royal Government of Cambodia on the morning of December 5, 2019, Mr. Ngin Chhay claimed that China, a major market for Cambodian mangoes, has given the green light to import. Pi came to inspect the mango plantation in early November.
Mr. Ngin Chhay confirmed that " Recently, on November 23, 24 and 25, officials of the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Customs Administration of the People's Republic of China visited the mango farms in Cambodia. Select 5 provinces for evaluation. "After a proud evaluation, the two experts concluded that the mango farms grown in Cambodia are in line with the standards that can be exported to China."
However, Chinese officials also noted that there are still some problems for Cambodian mangoes, such as the hygiene in the mango plantation is not clean, there is no mechanism to control the composition of pests, especially pests according to the season clearly and The use of some pesticides is not yet in accordance with the prescribed procedures.
To solve the remaining problem, it is necessary to establish a factory or location for proper packaging and disinfection, especially to kill worms and flies. Unless a factory or a slaughterhouse is established, fresh mangoes cannot be exported to China. This was confirmed by Mr. Ngin Chhay, Director General of the General Department of Agriculture.
Regarding the mango disinfection factory to be able to export fresh mangoes to China is a big challenge today because Cambodia has only one disinfection plant, Hyundai is located in Kampong Speu. The factory was completed in 2019 and has the capacity to kill between 25 and 30 tons of mangoes per day.
The Chinese Embassy in Cambodia claims that the Chinese government has opened up the market for Cambodian mangoes, which can import about 500,000 tonnes a year. In addition to China, South Korea has recently given the green light for Cambodian mangoes.
Although markets in China and South Korea have given the green light for Cambodia's fresh mangoes, it is clear that if Cambodia fails to set up more mango disinfection plants and packaging plants, Cambodia will still be hampered. Mango exports and mangoes are still piled up during the mango harvest season.
Ngin Chhay did not know the amount of money invested in the mango disinfection plant, but he did know that the equipment and equipment for the installation would cost around $ 500,000. In addition, the Ministry of Agriculture has opened talks with several Chinese companies to invest in setting up mango disinfection plants for export.
To answer the question, does the Royal Government plan to open any special investment to establish a mango disinfection plant so as not to lose the opportunity to export mangoes? Mr. Ngin Chhay claimed that in the past, only with small development partners, there was no special investment from the Royal Government.
Separately, Mr. Phay Siphan, Minister Delegate to the Prime Minister and Chairman of the Royal Government Spokesperson Unit, said that this investment is the Royal Government gives high priority to the private sector, but the Royal Government of Cambodia, led by Samdech Techo Hun Sen, is not indifferent. No effort will be made to find a mechanism to resolve this issue.
Mr. Ngin Chhay also called on the private sector and development partners to invest in mango processing and disinfection infrastructure so that Cambodia can achieve its goal of exporting at least 500,000 to 600,000 tons of fresh mangoes by 2023. Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon Chairs Inter-Ministerial Meeting in Preparation for the 13th Asia-Europe Summit
(Phnom Penh): On December 5, 2019, Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon, Minister of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation, chaired an inter-ministerial meeting at the Ministry of Foreign Affairs to prepare for the 13th Asia-Europe Meeting (ASEM). 2020, which Cambodia will host.
The Asia-Europe Meeting (ASEM) is a formal process discussion and cooperation between the 30 EU member states and 21 Asian countries, as well as the ASEAN Secretariat. The ASEM talks address political, economic and cultural issues with the aim of enhancing relations between the two regions on the basis of mutual respect and equal partnership.
The ASEM Summit is a biennial meeting of the Heads of State and Government, the President of the European Council, the President of the European Commission and the Secretary-General of the Association of Southeast Asian Nations (ASEAN). The Asia-Europe Meeting, held regularly every two years, has become an important forum for partnership cooperation in maintaining peace, stability, social and economic development.
Two mosques costing nearly $ 700,000 to appear in Koh Thom district, Kandal province
(Phnom Penh): Under the guidance of Mr. Hing Bun Heang, Mr. Chao Tol, Deputy Chairman of Koh Thom District Permanent Strengthening Committee, visited Prek Thmey Commune, Koh Thom District, Kandal Province, and Mr. Hoseen Mohamad Farit, President of the Council for the Development of Cambodia visited the mosque. Two places.
The first site is located in Cham Krom village, Prek Thmey commune, Koh Thom district, Kandal province, with a size of 35 meters by 20 meters, costing 600,000 USD, and the second location is in Tuol Sangke village, Tuol Kong commune, Koh Thom district. Kandal province is 15 meters by 20 meters, costing $ 70,000 from a Malaysian philanthropist through the Cambodia Development Council.
Addressing the Cambodian Muslims, Mr. Chao Tol brought a message from the leadership of the Cambodian People's Party, with Samdech Techo Hun Sen, President of the Cambodian People's Party, greeting the local people, even though Samdech did not come in person. .
Mr. Chao Tol said that Cambodia today, under the leadership of the Cambodian People's Party, has led the country to progress in all areas, regardless of political affiliation, religion, color or race. In fact, the study to build this mosque now shows more clearly the religious harmony in Cambodia without any religious discrimination.
The Deputy Chairman of the Working Group stated that the donation of these two churches was an encouragement to the Cambodian Muslims who supported and loved the Cambodian People's Party, especially the support of Samdech Techo Hun Sen in the past. Islam in the whole of Koh Thom district also has the attention of the working group of the Cambodian People's Party as well as organizations like the early rain.
In addition to inspecting the sites for the construction of new churches for the two Cambodian Muslims, Mr. Chao Tol also inspected the construction of two other schools donated by Samdech Techo Hun Sen and Samdech Kittipritthbandit Bun Rany. Hun Sen will inspect the mosque under construction, which is scheduled to be inaugurated in the near future under the presidency of Samdech Techo.
