SGMC is fully committed to building the first and best physical and mental health care destination for Cambodia.
To reduce traffic congestion, some roads will be made unidirectional, some roads will not be parked, some roads will be for buses
(Phnom Penh): The National Committee for Road Safety has been conducting a special study to reduce congestion in Phnom Penh today. To alleviate congestion, some roads will be closed to one-way traffic, while others will be off-road and others will be for bus use only. In addition, there will also be studies to create special lanes for public buses.
Senior Minister Sun Chanthol said that in order to help reduce traffic congestion in Phnom Penh, JICA has already helped install many smart traffic lights. With this traffic light, it can sense itself where there is a lot of congestion, which can help delay further on its own.
To reduce traffic congestion, Phnom Penh Capital Hall has also increased the number of buses to 235 with a capacity of 15,000 passengers per day. There are up to 13 bus lines to make it easier for people to use the bus service more.
Senior Minister Sun Chanthol said that Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng, Deputy Prime Minister, Minister of Interior and Chairman of the National Road Safety Committee, decided to set up a special working group to study traffic accident reduction in Phnom Penh. Some already.
Senior Minister Sun Chanthol also urged all people traveling on the streets to respect all traffic laws and stop properly in their path. Drive carefully, do not overtake on narrow roads and do not violate traffic lights that can cause harm to yourself and others and cause traffic congestion.
