(Phnom Penh): Deputy Prime Minister Chea Sophara, Minister of Land Management, Urban Planning and Construction, on December 5, 2019, stated that in 2019, the investment in the construction sector throughout the country is 9.3 billion. The US dollar has increased by 78 percent compared to 2018.
This confirmation was made on the occasion of Deputy Prime Minister Chea Sophara presiding over the 8th Annual General Meeting of the Association and the Opening Ceremony of the Cambodia-International Construction Industry Fair 2019 organized by the Association Build Cambodia. The event took place for 3 days from December 5-7, 2019 at the Koh Pich Exhibition Center.
Deputy Prime Minister Chea Sophara stated, "Investment in the construction sector in 2019 amounted to $ 9.3 billion nationwide, and compared with the investment recorded in 2018, it has increased to 78 percent.
At the same time, Deputy Prime Minister Chea Sophara also reminded all professionals and business people in the construction industry to respect the construction laws and regulations related to the construction sector properly to ensure the safety and quality of construction in Cambodia, such as:
* First: Construction permit and site permit must be obtained prior to construction and must comply with approved technical plans and other technical regulations.
* Second: Construction companies and design projects, including all construction workers must apply for registration as a business in accordance with the law in force.
* Third: The construction site must be organized in an orderly and hygienic manner and all construction sites must transport concrete, soil and construction materials to clear public roads.
* Fourth: All construction sites must be equipped with materials and equipment to protect the safety and health of all workers working in the construction site, etc.
* Fifth: All construction sites must have engineers, architects and site managers on a regular technical inspection of the construction site.
Deputy Prime Minister Chea Sophara further stated that all constructions must have a study of architectural design, structural design, plumbing, electrical and mechanical design, as well as a geological study in accordance with technical standards.
