The new sub-decree will help increase the salaries of civil servants to improve the daily lives of civil servants who work hard to serve the motherland.
The study found that free trade between Cambodia and the EU-Asia Economic Union is very profitable for the Cambodian economy.
The launch of the study was chaired by Minister of Commerce Pan Sosak and Professor Hidetoshi Nishimura, President of the Economic Research Institute for ASEAN and East Asia (ERIA). The event was also attended by officials from relevant ministries and institutions of the Royal Government, the private sector, development partners, international organizations and research institutes, a total of about 80 people.
The EU-Asia Economic Union is a large market covering Eastern Europe with five members: Armenia, Belarus, Kazakhstan, Kyrgyzstan and Russia. The EU-Asia Economic Union has a population of 176 million and a GDP of $ 2.5 trillion in 2016.
Mr. Pan Sosak said that by establishing a free trade agreement between Cambodia and the EU-Asia Economic Union, both sides will be able to increase economic and trade cooperation on goods, services and investment. The Minister said that the free trade agreement between Cambodia and the EU-Asia Economic Union has a positive effect on the Cambodian economy and allows Cambodia to reap the benefits by participating in regional and global production chains.
The report shows that the free trade agreement between Cambodia and the EU-Asia Economic Union will lead to growth in manufacturing, expansion of services and further development of new services. The study also shows that the expansion of financial services, transportation, the digital economy and agricultural services are important sectors that contribute to economic growth.
Through this free trade agreement, it is expected to increase opportunities for mutual trade and investment. In particular, Cambodia can increase exports of garments and textiles, agricultural products and other goods, while the EU-Asia Economic Union can also penetrate the wider Cambodian market, especially in the energy sector.
Minister Pan Sosak stated that in accordance with the Rectangular Strategy, Phase 4, Legislature of the Royal Government of Cambodia, which focuses on the establishment of bilateral free trade agreements with partner countries, the Ministry of Commerce has been studying the possibility of negotiating agreements with countries. China and South Korea as well.
The results of the study show that the positive cooperation in the free trade agreement will lead to an increase in Cambodia's trade volume, increase employment for the people, and achieve the Royal Government's plan to become a rich country. High middle income by 2030 and high income by 2050.
