Deputy PM Hor Namhong Reminds Phnom Penh's Transformation from Ghost Town to Capital Development in All Fields
(Phnom Penh): Deputy Prime Minister Hor Namhong reminded the AIMF General Assembly about the changes in Phnom Penh from the past to the present, after the end of the war, Phnom Penh has become a ghost town. Mouth came to be a prosperous and developed capital in all fields under the leadership of Samdech Techo Hun Sen.
This statement was made at the opening ceremony of the 39th General Assembly of the International Association of French-Speaking Mayors (AIMF), which Cambodia will officially host this morning, December 4, 2019 under the theme " Phnom Penh 40 years later.
According to Deputy Prime Minister Hor Namhong, the historic date of December 2, which led Cambodia to move towards the end of the Khmer Rouge genocidal regime on January 7, 1979 and, in other words, ended the civil war. Allow the revival and modernization of Phnom Penh as it is today.
The Deputy Prime Minister emphasized that at that time, the Royal Government had to feed and provide shelter to the millions of starving people who had just returned from forced labor camps, and we had to build all the urban infrastructure to live up to the reputation of a capital This, as well as other infrastructure throughout the country, was completely destroyed by the Khmer Rouge genocidal regime.
Since 2000, the city has faced new emergencies, with people leaving the countryside to relocate, requiring the construction of temporary housing in some neighborhoods. Since 2004, the city has begun to expand its reach, beginning with the industrialization phase, creating more jobs in the textile industry.
The Royal Government has an obligation to set out two strategies:
