Ministry of Environment Spokesman: Plastic waste is a global challenge and concern
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State and Spokesman of the Ministry of Environment, emphasized that the problem of plastic waste is a challenge and a common concern of the world. Fighting plastic waste pollution is a difficult task that requires the participation and efforts of all stakeholders, including relevant authorities, citizens, associations and national and international NGOs, the private sector, development partners and Stakeholders.
He said that from the first plastic production until 2017, more than 8.3 billion tons of plastic waste, and only 9% of plastic waste is recycled. Plastic waste is increasing every year, by 2050, plastic waste will reach 34 billion tons. By 2050, there may be more plastic than fish in the sea if no effective prevention measures are taken.
It is estimated that the percentage of plastic collected through street cleaning in Siem Reap is 20.7% and 34% in Sihanoukville. According to the study, one city resident consumes more than 2,000 plastic bags a year. Nationwide, waste is growing at about 10% a year. The growth of waste is
Mr. Neth Pheaktra explained that due to population growth, economic growth, lifestyle changes, packaging changes, people are turning to simple packaging items such as plastic bags. Cambodia is classified as a country with rapid economic growth, with a growth rate of around 7% per year and rapid development, the problem of waste has also increased steadily.
2018 data show that the total solid waste collected at the field is about 1,709,379 tons, compared to 2017, an increase of 205.656 tons, equivalent to 13.68%. This growth problem also contributes to lifestyle changes, food packaging and a lack of understanding of how to properly dispose of and dispose of waste. Currently, waste is collected, collected and dumped in landfills without being recycled, recycled, especially plastic waste.
