The fire in the board factory in Preah Sihanouk province has been extinguished, the authorities use 5 water trucks to put out the fire
(Preah Sihanouk): The case of the fire at the board factory in the closed economic zone of Preah Sihanouk province was extinguished at noon on December 3, 2019.
Preah Sihanouk Provincial Police Commissioner Major General Chuon Narin said that in the case of the fire, the police used 5 fire trucks, including 3 police, 1 military and 1 Sihanoukville Autonomous Port.
For many years, Egypt has suffered from a water crisis caused mainly by population growth, and Egyptian farmers have worked hard to take advantage of the limited water resources they have access to. In addition, there are a number of other factors, including global warming and water pollution. Now, even more worrying is the near-completion of Ethiopia's dam on Africa's longest Nile River.
Egypt considers the dam a major threat that could deplete its water resources "at a very serious level", although the Ethiopian side has assured that water resources for Egypt will not be severely affected. However, Egypt on Monday resumed talks with Sudan and Ethiopia over the dam project, with an agreement set to be reached before January 2020.
Nearly 97 percent of Egypt's fresh water comes from the Nile, and its banks are home to some 95 million Egyptians. This is according to the UN report. Construction of Ethiopia's dam began in 2011 as it is expected to start generating power by the end of 2020 and be fully operational by 2022.
According to Mr. Eang Sophalleth, Samdech Techo Hun Sen's personal assistant, during the meeting, Samdech Techo Hun Sen and Ms. Lewis Musikivabo discussed in detail the promotion of cooperation and relations between the French-speaking countries.
She emphasized that her visit to the French-speaking Mayors' Conference was aimed at boosting relations between Cambodia and the Francophone International Organization and promoting more specific projects.
During the meeting, Ms. Louise Musiki Kiwobo asked Cambodia to implement a number of specific projects to strengthen relations and cooperation between French-speaking countries, such as promoting French language teaching, promoting trade between French-speaking countries and celebrating the 50th anniversary. Year of the founding of the French-speaking international organization in 2020.
As the founder of the French-speaking International Organization, Samdech Techo Hun Sen emphasized that Cambodia will continue to be active within the framework of the French-speaking International Organization. Cambodia will also be co-hosting the 50th anniversary of the founding of the French-speaking international organization next year. Cambodia may arrange for Cambodian students to learn French in France for various activities to celebrate the 50th anniversary.
In this morning's meeting, Ms. Lewis Musiki Kibobo also congratulated Cambodia for turning from a country that used to receive UN assistance to find peace to a country that sends peacekeepers after the United Nations umbrella. Nations to help countries in crisis by war.
She encouraged Cambodia to become more involved in peacekeeping operations under the auspices of the United Nations.
It should be noted that at the invitation of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Ms. Lewis Musikivabo, Secretary General of the International Organization for Francophone (OIF) led a delegation to pay a four-day official visit to Cambodia from 2 to 5 December 2019. .
This is the first official visit by Ms. Lewis Musikivabo to strengthen relations between Cambodia and the OIF after she took office as Secretary-General on January 3, 2019.
During the visit, Ms. Lewis Musiki Kiwabo will also visit Siem Reap. The Secretary-General will be welcomed by Siem Reap Provincial Governor Tea Seiha and visit the Angkor Wat temples.
She will also attend a regional workshop on research activities organized by the Francophone Asia-Pacific Regional Center (CREFAP) and the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports, and visit bilingual classes at Wat Bo Primary School.
The OIF is now the largest intergovernmental organization in the world (after the United Nations) with a total of 88 member states and governments (54 full members, 7 non-members and 27 observer members). .
